hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"4236","From Mamar and al-Thawri, Sulayman al-Taymi said: from Abu Mijlaz, he said: I said to Ibn Umar: I caught a rakah of the residents prayer while I am a traveler? He said: Pray according to their prayer."
"4237","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan concerning a traveler who catches a rakah of the residents prayer in Zuhr: He said: He adds three to it; and if he catches them sitting, he prays two rakahs."
"4238","From Abu Hanifah, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: When you join a people, pray according to their prayer."
"4239","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah concerning a traveler catching a rakah of the residents prayer: They said: He prays according to their prayer; but if he catches them sitting, he prays two rakahs."
"4240","From Mamar, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, and from Amr, from al-Hasan: They said: When he catches them sitting, he prays two rakahs."
"4241","From Mamar and al-Thawri: They said: When he catches them sitting, he prays according to their prayer. From Mamar, from a man, from Ikrimah, the like of the saying of al-Zuhri and Qatadah."
"4242","From al-Thawri, he said: Whoever forgets a prayer in residence and remembers it on a journey prays four; and if he forgets a prayer on a journey and remembers it in residence, he prays two rakahs."
"4243","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan saying: Whoever forgets a residence prayer until he travels prays it as four; and if he forgets a prayer on a journey until he comes to residence, he prays four. And Hammad said: He prays two rakahs. And the saying of al-Hasan is dearer to Mamar: He completes, so that he is not in doubt."
"4244","From Mamar, from Hammad, he said: If he forgets a residence prayer and remembers while traveling, he prays four."
"4245","From Mamar, from Qatadah: Concerning a man who out of ignorance prayed Maghrib as two rakahs, two rakahs until he returned: He said: He repeats what he remembers."
"4246","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when travel pressed him would combine Maghrib and Isha. From Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, the like of it. From Malik, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"4247","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Hafs ibn Ubaydillah ibn Anas, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would combine Zuhr and Asr, and Maghrib and Isha, on a journey."
"4248","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combined Zuhr and Asr on a journey in daytime."
"4249","From Malik, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combined Zuhr and Asr, and Maghrib and Isha, in his expedition to Tabuk."
"4250","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Abu al-Tufayl, that Muadh ibn Jabal said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combined Zuhr and Asr, and Maghrib and Isha, in the expedition of Tabuk."
"4251","From Malik, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Abu al-Tufayl, that Muadh ibn Jabal informed them that they went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Tabuk. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would combine Zuhr and Asr, and Maghrib and Isha. He said: So he delayed the prayer one day, then came out and prayed Zuhr and Asr together, then came out and prayed Maghrib and Isha together, then said: You will come, if Allah wills, tomorrow to the spring of Tabuk, and you will come to it in the forenoon; whoever of you comes to it, let him not touch any of its water until I come. He said: So we came to it, and two men had already come to it, and the spring was like a shoelace trickling a little water. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked them: Did you touch any of its water? They said: Yes. He said: So he reviled them, and said to them what Allah willed him to say. Then they scooped from the spring with their hands a little until it gathered in a vessel, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) washed in it his face and his hands, then returned it into it, and the spring flowed with abundant water, and the people drew water. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Soon, O Muadh, if your life is long, you will see this place filled with gardens."
"4252","From Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar informed me that Safiyyah bint Abi Ubayd, his wife, was dying. He said: He traveled until it grew dark and we thought he had forgotten. He said: We kept saying: The prayer! while he would not answer us, until about a quarter of the night had gone, the time the laden ones take from Arafah to Muzdalifah, then he alighted and prayed Maghrib, then turned to us and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when travel pressed him, or resolved him to travel, would combine these two prayers, then he prayed Isha."
"4253","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me, he said: Ibn Umar combined the two prayers one time. He said: News came to him about Safiyyah bint Abi Ubayd that she was ill, so he set out after praying Asr, then hastened the journey; he traveled until the time of Maghrib came, and a man of his companions spoke to him, saying: The prayer! but he did not turn to him. Then another spoke to him but he did not turn to him, and another spoke to him but he did not return anything to him, then another spoke to him, and he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when hastening, delay this prayer until he combined the two prayers."
"4254","From Mamar, from Ayyub and Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar was told of his wife illness while on a journey, so he delayed Maghrib; it was said to him: The prayer! but he was silent and delayed it past the disappearance of the twilight until a stretch of the night had gone, then he alighted and prayed Maghrib and Isha, then said: This is how the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would do when travel pressed him."
"4255","From Mamar, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar would pray every prayer on a journey at its time, except one prayer when he was told of his wife illness, for he combined Maghrib and Isha; it was said to him, and he said: This is how the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would do; when travel pressed him he would combine Maghrib and Isha. And it was in some of their hadith: he delayed them both together until a quarter of the night."
"4256","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah, from Ata and Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would combine the two prayers on a journey, Zuhr and Asr, and Maghrib and Isha, without pursuing an enemy nor being pursued by an enemy."
"4257","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Husayn ibn Abdillah ibn Abbas informed me, from Ikrimah and from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Shall I not tell you about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey? We said: Yes. He said: When the sun had declined for him at his stop, he would combine Zuhr and Asr before riding; and when it had not declined for him at his stop, he would travel until when Asr came he alighted and combined Zuhr and Asr; and when Maghrib came upon him while at his stop, he would combine it with Isha; and when it had not come upon him at his stop, he would ride until when Isha came he alighted and combined them. He said: And al-Miqdam said to me: We never heard this from Ibn Jurayj, nor did anyone but you bring it."
"4258","From Mamar, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, he said: I accompanied Sad ibn Abi Waqqas from Kufah to Makkah, setting out as envoys, and Sad would combine Zuhr and Asr, and Maghrib and Isha, bringing a little of this forward and delaying a little of this, until we came to Makkah."
"4259","From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman, he said: Said ibn Zayd and Usamah went out, and they would combine Zuhr and Asr, and Maghrib and Isha."
"4260","From Mamar, from Tawus, from his father, that Ibn Abbas said: We would combine Zuhr and Asr on a journey."
"4261","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that Ibn Abbas combined Maghrib and Isha on the night he left his land. He said: Whoever combined them would delay Zuhr a little and hasten Asr a little, then they are joined; and delay Maghrib and hasten Isha, then they are joined."
"4262","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if a man prays them at the time of one of them, does it harm him? He said: It does not harm him."
"4263","From Ibn Jurayj and Zamah ibn Salih, from Ibn Tawus, he said: Tawus would combine the two prayers from al-Janad until he reached Makkah, and he would pray between them and with them what he used to pray in residence."
"4264","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: When people are on a journey and no stop is convenient for them, they travel until they reach the stop and delay a little, then alight and combine the two prayers; and when they linger at the stop, likewise."
"4265","From Mamar, from Amr ibn Qatadah, he said: I was a hired hand of Salim ibn Abdillah; he would set out from Madinah to Makkah, and Salim would order his women to combine Zuhr and Asr, then I would travel with them, while he would stay behind at the stop, I do not know what he does."
"4266","From Malik, from Ibn Shihab, he said: I asked Salim ibn Abdillah: May Zuhr and Asr be combined on a journey? He said: There is no harm in that; have you not seen the prayer of the people at Arafah?"
"4267","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, he said: A woman came to Tawus and said: I dislike my father, he made me combine the two prayers. He said: It does not harm you; do you not see that the people combine midday and Asr at Arafah, and Maghrib and Isha at Muzdalifah?"
"4268","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Umm Dharrah, from Aishah, that she would order women to combine the two prayers on a journey."
"4269","From Mamar, he said: I heard that the prayer was combined because of His saying: Establish prayer from the decline of the sun to the darkness of the night; so the darkness of the night is Maghrib and Isha."
"4270","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: People who are not on Hajj, nor Umrah, nor a raid, may they combine the two prayers? He said: Yes; glory be to Allah! I perform tawaf here seven times, then I pray Isha, or seventy times."
"4271","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, he said: My father would alight watching the sun until Asr came."
"4272","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Ibn Masud, he said: I never saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray a prayer except at its time, except that he combined Zuhr and Asr at Arafah, and Maghrib and Isha at Muzdalifah. From Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Amash, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Ibn Masud, the like of it. He said: And he prayed Fajr that day before its time."
"4273","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Aliyah, that Umar wrote to Abu Musa: Know that combining two prayers is among the major sins, except with an excuse."
"4274","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Ayyub and Qatadah, from Abu al-Aliyah, that he would pray on a journey every prayer at its time."
"4275","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from al-Aswad, he said: He would alight for the time of every prayer, even if he alighted upon a stone."
"4276","From Mamar, from Jabir al-Jufi, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from his father, that he would pray every prayer at its time on a journey."
"4277","From Hisham, from al-Hasan, that he would say: Pray every prayer at its time. From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan, saying the like of that."
"4278","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Makhul: that he disliked combining two prayers on a journey."
"4279","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim: They do not combine on a journey, nor pray but two rakahs."
"4280","From Hammam, from Harun ibn Qays, from Salim ibn Abdillah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah have mercy on Abdullah ibn Rawahah; he would alight on a journey at the time of every prayer."
"4281","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, from Salim, he said: I said: What is the farthest Ibn Umar delayed Maghrib? He said: From Dhat al-Jaysh to Dhat al-Sufuq, and between them are eight miles."
"4282","From Ibrahim ibn Yazid, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the sun set for him while he was at Sarif, but he did not pray Maghrib until he entered Makkah. And al-Hajjaj ibn Artah mentioned the like of it from Abu al-Zubayr."
"4283","From Yahya ibn Abdillah and others, that Wahb ibn Munabbih, the sun would set for him while he was at the village of al-Rahabah, and he would mount his animal until he came to his dwelling in Sanaa."
"4284","From Dawud ibn Qays, from Salih, freedman of al-Tawamah, that he heard Ibn Abbas saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combined Zuhr and Asr, and Maghrib and Isha, in Madinah without a journey or rain. He said: I said to Ibn Abbas: Why do you think he did that? He said: I think, to make room for his ummah."
"4285","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combined Zuhr and Asr in Madinah without a journey or fear. He said: I said to Ibn Abbas: Why do you think he did that? He said: He intended not to burden anyone of his ummah."
"4286","He said: Ibn Jurayj and Mamar informed us, from Amr ibn Dinar, that Abu al-Shatha informed him that Ibn Abbas informed him, he said: I prayed behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) eight together and seven together in Madinah. Ibn Jurayj said: So I said to Abu al-Shatha: I think the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delayed Zuhr a little and brought Asr forward a little. Abu al-Shatha said: And I think that."
"4287","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: Abdullah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combined for us, resident and not traveling, Zuhr and Asr and Maghrib. A man said to Ibn Umar: Why do you think the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did that? He said: So that his ummah would not be burdened, if a man combines."
"4288","From Malik, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: When the rulers combined the two prayers, Maghrib and Isha, in the rain, he would combine with them."
"4289","From Dawud ibn Qays, he said: I heard Raja ibn Haywah asking Nafi: Did Ibn Umar combine with the people between the two prayers when they combined on the rainy night? He said: Yes."
"4290","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab combined Zuhr and Asr on a rainy day."
"4291","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, that the people of Madinah would combine Maghrib and Isha on the rainy night, and Ibn Umar would pray with them, not disapproving of that."
"4292","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if I combine the two prayers on a journey, does it suffice me not to speak between them? He said: As for me, I like to separate between them."
"4293","From Abdullah ibn Umar, he said: Isa ibn Asim narrated to me, from his father, he said: Ibn Umar prayed a daytime prayer on a journey, and saw some of them praying nafl, so Ibn Umar said: What are they doing? It was said to him: They are praying nafl. He said: Had I been one to pray nafl, I would have completed the prayer. I performed Hajj with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he would not pray nafl in the daytime; and I performed Hajj with Abu Bakr and he would not pray nafl in the daytime; and I performed Hajj with Umar and he would not pray nafl in the daytime; and I performed Hajj with Uthman and he would not pray nafl in the daytime. Then Ibn Umar said: There has certainly been for you in the Messenger of Allah an excellent example."
"4294","From Israil ibn Yunus, from Thuwayr ibn Abi Fakhitah, that Ali would not pray voluntary prayers on a journey, neither before them nor after them."
"4295","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: that he would not pray voluntary prayers on a journey, and he would say: Had I prayed voluntarily, I would have completed; and he would pray the night nafl on a journey."
"4296","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, he said: Ibn Umar would pray nafl at night, but not in the daytime on a journey, and he would pray toward his camel."
"4297","From Mamar, from Qatadah and Ayyub, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar would not pray voluntary prayers on a journey in the daytime prayers."
"4298","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar would not pray the two rakahs of Fajr on a journey, nor leave them in residence."
"4299","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, from Abdullah ibn Waqid, he said: Ibn Umar would not pray the two rakahs of Fajr on a journey, nor leave them in residence."
"4300","From Israil, from Thuwayr ibn Abi Fakhitah, he said: I accompanied Mujahid on journeys repeatedly, and he would not pray nafl neither before them nor after them."
"4301","From Mamar, he said: I traveled with Ayyub, and he would not pray nafl for Zuhr and Asr at all, not adding to two rakahs, two rakahs, except that he would pray two rakahs before Fajr, and two rakahs after Maghrib, and he would pray some rakahs after Isha, and he would pray witr before sleeping."
"4302","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: I said: When I travel and shorten the prayer, may I pray before it if I wish, or after it? He said: Yes; I take the concession and the Sunnah, so I shorten; then I love increase of good, so I pray nafl."
"4303","From Ibn Jurayj and Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he would pray nafl on a journey as he prayed nafl in residence, and he would combine the two prayers."
"4304","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, that Umar and Ibn Masud would pray on a journey before the prescribed prayer and after it. Abd al-Razzaq said: And I myself saw al-Thawri doing it."
"4305","From Israil, from Isa ibn Abi Azzah, he said: I saw Amir al-Shabi praying nafl on a journey before them and after them."
"4306","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, he said: I saw Makhul praying nafl on a journey before them and after them."
"4307","And I myself saw al-Thawri praying nafl before them and after them."
"4308","From Mamar, from Ayyub, he said: I myself saw al-Qasim ibn Muhammad praying nafl on a journey, and I saw Salim not praying nafl."
"4309","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I know of none of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who would pray the full prayer on a journey except Sad ibn Abi Waqqas. He said: And Aishah would complete the prayer on a journey and fast. He said: And Sad ibn Abi Waqqas traveled with a group of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Sad completed the prayer and fasted, while the people shortened and broke fast. They said to Sad: How do they break fast and shorten while you complete and fast? He said: Mind your own affair, for I know my own case best. He said: Sad did not forbid it to them nor prohibit them from it."
"4310","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Which of that is dearer to you? He said: Shortening it; and all that the righteous and the best have done."
"4311","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, he said: She would fast on a journey and pray four, or he said: and complete."
"4312","From al-Thawri, from Hisham, from Urwah, from Aishah: that she would complete on a journey."
"4313","From Ibn Muharrar, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Aishah, she said: Whoever prays four on a journey, it is good; and whoever prays two, it is good. Allah will not punish you for addition, but He punishes you for deficiency."
"4314","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from Abu Qilabah, he said: He would say: If you pray four on a journey, then one against whom there is no objection has prayed."
"4315","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: A man came to Ibn Umar and said: I and a companion of mine were on a journey; I completed and he shortened. Ibn Umar said: Rather he completed, and you shortened."
"4316","From Ghalib ibn Ubaydillah, he said: Hammad informed me, from Ibrahim, that Ibn Masud said: Whoever prays four on a journey repeats the prayer."
"4317","Ghalib said: And al-Sakhtiyani informed me of that, that Ibn Abbas said: Allah sent down the prayer in full, and He prescribed for the traveler a prayer and for the resident a prayer; so it is not proper for the resident to pray the traveler prayer, nor for the traveler to pray the resident prayer."
"4318","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Safwan ibn Abdillah, from Umm al-Darda, from Kab ibn Asim al-Ashari, and he was of the companions of the ship, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Fasting on a journey is not righteousness."
"4319","From Mamar, he said: Al-Zuhri would say: The traveler breaks fast when he goes far, and that is a journey of two days."
"4320","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me that Safwan ibn Abdillah ibn Safwan narrated to him, from Umm al-Darda, from Kab ibn Asim al-Ashari, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fasting on a journey is not righteousness."
"4321","From Abd al-Wahhab, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd informed me, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fasting on a journey is not righteousness."
"4322","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydillah ibn Abdillah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out in the year of the Conquest in the month of Ramadan; he fasted until he reached al-Kadid, then broke fast. Al-Zuhri said: So breaking fast was the later of the two matters."
"4323","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, from Ubaydillah ibn Abdillah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out in the year of the Conquest in the month of Ramadan; he fasted until he reached al-Kadid, then broke fast. He said: So they would follow the latest of the commands of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the latest of his commands."
"4324","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out in the year of the Conquest in the month of Ramadan; he fasted until he passed a pool on the road, at midday; he said: The people thirsted and stretched their necks, their souls longing for it. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for a cup with water in it, held it on his hand until the people saw him, then drank, and the people drank."
"4325","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Jafar ibn Muhammad informed me, from his father, he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at Usfan, or at al-Kadid, Abd al-Malik was unsure, on his way out for the Conquest, a cup was handed to him while he was on his mount in the month of Ramadan; the companies passed by him with the cup on his hand, then he drank. It reached him afterward that some people fasted, so he said: Those are the disobedient, three times."
"4326","From Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar would not fast on a journey, nor pray more than two rakahs in the daytime, and he would keep the night alive."
"4327","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, he said: I never saw Ibn Umar fast on a journey except one day; I saw him break fast when evening came, so we said: Were you fasting? He said: Yes; I thought I would enter Makkah today, so I disliked that the people be fasting while I am breaking fast, and that was in Ramadan."
"4328","From Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, from Ismail ibn Rafi, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a man asked him about prayer and breaking fast in the month of Ramadan on a journey, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Break fast. He said: I am strong enough to fast, O Messenger of Allah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Are you stronger, or Allah? Allah has given as charity the breaking of the fast to the sick of my ummah and its travelers. Would one of you like that one of you be given charity, then he keeps returning it to him?"
"4329","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from a man of Banu Amir, that a man called Anas narrated to him that he came to Madinah and entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a need, and found the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) eating. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Come near. The man said: I am fasting. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The traveler has been relieved of fasting and half the prayer, and the pregnant and the nursing. From Abdullah ibn Muharrar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from a man of Banu Amir, from a man called Anas, the like of the hadith of Mamar."
"4330","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: I was with him when people of al-Jazirah came to him and said: O Abu Muhammad, we travel in litters, and we are spared hardship, shall we fast? He said: No. They said: We are strong enough for that. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was stronger and better than you. He said: Your best are those who, when they travel, shorten the prayer and do not fast."
"4331","From Abu Said ibn Habib, that Urwah ibn Ruwaym narrated to him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of my ummah are those who testify there is no god but Allah, alone without partner, and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger; and those who, when they do good, rejoice, and when they do evil, seek forgiveness; and when they travel, shorten and break fast. And the worst of my ummah are those born in luxury and raised in it; their concern, or he said: their business, is soft clothing, fine food, and obscenity in speech."
"4332","From Yasin ibn Abi Bistam, from Dahhak ibn Muzahim, he said: Ibn Abbas said to me: Whatever you disobey me in, do not disobey me in three things: when you go out traveling, pray two rakahs until you return to your family; do not fast until you return to your house; and do not enter Makkah except in ihram."
"4333","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Asim ibn Ubaydillah, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab ordered a man who fasted the month of Ramadan on a journey to make it up. Ibn Uyaynah said: And Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Kulthum ibn Jabr, from a man, from Umar, the like of it."
"4334","From Yahya ibn Rabiah, he said: I asked Ata ibn Abi Rabah about one fasting on a journey. He said: As for the obligatory, no; as for the voluntary, no harm."
"4335","From Hisham ibn Hassan, he said: I heard al-Qasim narrating from Ibn Umar, he said: He would say: Whoever accompanies us, let him not fast. He said: And he would not fast on a journey."
"4336","From Mamar, from al-Hasan and Qatadah: They said: The traveler breaks fast and shortens the prayer."
"4337","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: There was with him a man fasting on the day of travel; Ibn Umar would order his sahur to be prepared for him, and when it was time to break fast he would alight and wait for him until he broke fast. He said: One day the man suffered severe hardship from thirst, so Ibn Umar said to him: If you enter the Fire after what I see, I have indeed seen one free of blame."
"4338","From Mamar, from Ayyub, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz summoned Salim ibn Abdillah and Urwah ibn al-Zubayr and asked them about the traveler in Ramadan: does he fast or break fast? Urwah said: I only took from Aishah; and Salim said: I only took from Abdullah ibn Umar. When they disputed and their voices rose, Umar said: O Allah, forgive! O Allah, forgive! I fast it in ease, and break it in hardship."
"4339","From Muqatil, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb informed me, from his father, from his grandfather, from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) breaking fast and fasting."
"4340","And I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying barefoot and wearing sandals."
"4341","And I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drinking standing and sitting."
"4342","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fasted on a journey and broke fast, so neither one who fasts nor one who breaks fast is blamed. Whoever fasts is better than one who breaks fast. From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, the like of it; and he said: Take the easier of them for you. Allah, Blessed and Exalted, said: Allah intends for you ease and does not intend for you hardship."
"4343","From Israil ibn Yunus and Ashath ibn Abi al-Shatha, that they set out to Makkah with al-Aswad ibn Yazid with them; the crescent of Ramadan overtook them, so they fasted on the road. He said: And we passed Bir Maymun, and he ordered them to bathe."
"4344","From Israil, from Amir ibn Shaqiq, from Shaqiq ibn Salamah, he said: We sighted the crescent of Ramadan at Hulwan or al-Madain, with men of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among us; their commander called out: Whoever of you wishes to fast, let him fast; and whoever of you wishes to break fast, let him break fast, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fasted on a journey and broke fast."
"4345","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Misar, from al-Hasan ibn Sad, from his father, he said: I came with Ali ibn Abi Talib from Yanbu. He said: Ali fasted, while Ali was riding, and I broke fast because I was walking, until we arrived at Madinah at night; we passed the house of Uthman ibn Affan, and he was reciting. Ali stood listening to his recitation, then Ali said: He is reciting in a surah, or he said: in Surah al-Nahl. Abu Bakr said: I was told that between Yanbu and Madinah is four days."
"4346","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah: that she would fast on a journey."
"4347","From Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: Fasting was disliked for the traveler only because people say: Move on for him, for he is fasting; and feed his animal, for he is fasting."
"4348","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: We do not blame one who fasts on a journey nor one who breaks fast. Allah said: Allah intends for you ease and does not intend for you hardship."
"4349","From Mamar, from Humayd, from al-Araj, he said: I heard Mujahid saying: Take the easier of them for you; Allah intended nothing but ease."
"4350","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: Some of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fasted on a journey, and some broke fast, and neither blamed the other. He said: This one took the concession of Allah, and this one fulfilled the obligation of Allah."
"4351","From Ja'far ibn Sulayman, from Hisham, he said: Ibn Sirin used to fast one day and break fast one day, both in residence and on journey."
"4352","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, he said: A man came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: O Messenger of Allah, I used to fast continuously and I intend to travel. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: If you wish, fast, and if you wish, break your fast."
"4353","From Ibn Jurayj, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, that Hamzah al-Aslami asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about fasting while traveling, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: If you wish, fast, and if you wish, break your fast."
"4354","'Abd al-Razzaq informed us, he said: Ma'mar informed us, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, he said: When a man sets out traveling in the month of Ramadan, having begun the morning fasting, he may break his fast if he wishes when he sets out."
"4355","From Ma'mar, from one who heard al-Hasan saying: The fasting person should not break his fast that day except if severe thirst afflicts him; if he fears for himself, he breaks his fast."
"4356","From Isra'il, from Jabir, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: He should not break his fast on that day."
"4357","From Isra'il, from Jabir, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, that 'Amr ibn Shurahbil set out traveling during the day, and when he crossed the Euphrates he ordered his servant, who gave him drink, so he broke his fast."
"4358","From Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata, he said: A man should not pray the obligatory prayer on his mount, whether facing toward the House or turning away from it, except if he is sick or afraid; then let him pray on his mount facing toward the House, not turning away from it."
"4359","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to 'Ata: Travelers with rain ahead of them, may they pray on their mounts? He said: Yes, if they wish. I said: Do they perform dry ablution with dust if they find no water? He said: Yes."
"4360","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Thawban, from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when he intended to pray the obligatory prayer, would dismount from his camel and face the qiblah."
"4361","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Anas ibn Sirin, he said: I was with Anas ibn Malik on a rainy day until when we were at Atit and the ground was muddy and wet, he led us in 'Asr prayer on his donkey, gesturing with his head, and he made the prostration lower than the bowing."
"4362","From Ma'mar, from 'Asim al-Ahwal, he said: I heard Anas ibn Malik saying: He was traveling through water and mud, and the time of the obligatory prayer came and he could not get out of that water. He said: And we feared the prayer would be missed, so we sought Allah's guidance and faced the qiblah, and we gestured on our mounts with gestures."
"4363","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, he said: One who saw Abu al-Sha'tha' informed me that he would gesture in prayer amid water and mud."
"4364","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to pray on the backs of their mounts wherever they faced, except for the obligatory prayer and the witr."
"4365","From Mansur, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata, he said: If a person is in water and cannot get out of it, let him pray and gesture with his head, and not prostrate."
"4366","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to pray voluntary prayers on his mount wherever it turned with him, but when he intended to pray the obligatory prayer he would dismount from his mount and face the qiblah."
"4367","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amir ibn Rabi'ah informed me, from his father, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, praying on the back of his mount in every direction."
"4368","From 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, that he used to pray voluntary prayers on his mount wherever it turned with him, and he would inform them that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to do so. He said: I asked Nafi' how the witr was? He said: He used to perform witr on his mount, and sometimes he would dismount and perform witr on the ground."
"4369","From al-Thawri, from 'Amr ibn Yahya, he said: Sa'id ibn Yasar narrated to us, from Ibn 'Umar, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, praying voluntarily on his donkey while heading toward Khaybar."
"4370","From Ibn Mujahid, from his father, from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to pray voluntarily on his mount wherever it turned with him, and he would make the prostration lower than the bowing."
"4371","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, that he heard Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah saying: I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, praying while on his mount, the supererogatory prayers, in every direction, but he would lower the prostration below the bowing, gesturing with gestures."
"4372","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent me for a need, and I came while he was praying toward the east, gesturing with his head on his mount, the prostration lower than the bowing. So I greeted him and he did not return my greeting, and when he finished his prayer he said: What did you do about such-and-such need? I was praying."
"4373","From Malik, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, he said: I saw Anas ibn Malik on a journey, praying on a donkey while facing other than the qiblah, bowing and prostrating with gestures of his head, without placing his face on anything."
"4374","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, he said: I saw Anas ibn Malik praying voluntarily on his mount while heading toward Syria."
"4375","From Ibn Jurayj, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, he said: One whom I do not accuse informed me, from Ibn 'Umar, that he used to pray on his mount while traveling in the direction he faced."
"4376","From Ibn Jurayj, and Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: He prays on his mount in every direction."
"4377","From Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata, he said: A person prays on his mount with his back turned toward Syria and Yemen. He said: I said: Even if he is traveling for worldly business? He said: Yes. He begins by saying takbir, then recites, then bows, then prostrates, then recites the tashahhud."
"4378","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from 'Ata, he said: One prays on all mounts: on the camel, the horse, the mule, and the donkey. He said: I said: Even on the donkey? He said: Yes."
"4379","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from 'Ata: When you bow, place your hands on your knees, then bow lowering your head, then make the prostration lower than the bowing. I said: Like the bowing and prostration of a sick person? He said: Yes."
"4380","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to 'Ata: Has any established authority reached you about praying on the mount with one's back turned to the qiblah? He said: Yes. Then he said at that point: And to Allah belong the east and the west, so wherever you turn, there is the Face of Allah. Ibn Jurayj said: That was mentioned to Yahya ibn Ja'dah and he almost denied it, then he set out and found it widespread in Madinah, so he returned to us acknowledging it."
"4381","Ma'mar informed us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', that Ibn 'Umar used to pray voluntarily on his mount while traveling, wherever it turned with him."
"4382","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to 'Ata: May I perform witr while turned away from the qiblah on my mount? He said: Yes."
"4383","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Nafi' saying: Ibn 'Umar used to perform witr on his mount."
"4384","From 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, from Nafi', that Ibn 'Umar used to perform witr on his mount, and sometimes he would perform witr on the ground."
"4385","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, and Ayyub, from Nafi', that Ibn 'Umar used to perform witr on his mount."
"4386","From Abu Ma'shar, he said: I heard Nafi' saying: A man stayed behind while we were on a journey, and Ibn 'Umar said to him: What kept you behind? He said: I performed witr. He said: One better than you performed witr on a camel, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"4387","From Muqatil, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, performed witr on his mount."
"4388","From al-Thawri, from Thuwayr ibn Abi Fakhitah, from his father, he said: 'Ali used to perform witr on his mount."
"4389","From al-Thawri, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Awn, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, that 'Umar used to perform witr on the ground."
"4390","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, that Ibn 'Umar used to perform witr on his mount when it was the time before dawn, so he would pray the witr."
"4391","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, that Ibn 'Umar, when he intended to perform witr, would dismount from his mount and perform witr on the ground."
"4392","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to 'Ata: May a man perform witr while sitting? He said: Yes."
"4393","Ma'mar informed us, he said: I said to Qatadah: May a man pray while driving his mount toward other than the qiblah? He said: No, except if he is afraid."
"4394","Ma'mar said, and one who heard al-Hasan narrated to me, saying: A person prays likewise, but when he intends bowing and prostration he faces the qiblah. Ma'mar said: And the saying of al-Hasan is more pleasing to me."
"4395","From Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata, he said: I said to him: People on a ship, do they shorten? He said: No, except if they fear drowning. He said: I said: Whoever among them works in it, does he shorten? He said: Yes."
"4396","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It was narrated to me, from Anas ibn Malik: That he shortened on a ship and prayed in it sitting, and those with him prayed sitting."
"4397","From Hisham ibn Hassan, that Anas ibn Sirin informed him, he said: Anas ibn Malik led us in prayer on the ship sitting, on a mat, and he shortened the prayer."
"4398","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, that Ibn Sirin shortened on the ship, and when he arrived at Wasit he completed the prayer."
"4399","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, that Anas ibn Malik shortened on the ship."
"4400","From Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata, he said: They pray on the ship standing, except if they fear they will drown, then they pray sitting, following the qiblah wherever it shifts."
"4401","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to 'Ata: What if there is a shore near me, should I disembark and pray on it? He said: If you do not detain your companions, then yes."
"4402","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: Pray on the ship, and do not cause hardship to your companions."
"4403","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: One prays on the ship standing, and if unable, then sitting, following the qiblah wherever it inclines."
"4404","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: Pray on the ship if you wish standing, and if you wish sitting, prostrating on a firm part of it or on a mat."
"4405","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, and 'Asim ibn Sulayman, that Anas ibn Malik led his companions in prayer on the ship sitting, on a mat."
"4406","From al-Thawri, from Husayn, from Mujahid, he said: We used to pray on the ship sitting. From Hisham, from Anas ibn Sirin, the like of that."
"4407","From al-Thawri, from Humayd al-Tawil, from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi 'Utbah, he said: I was with Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, Abu al-Darda, and I think he mentioned Abu Hurayrah, on a ship, and the one who led us led us standing, though if we had wished to go out we could have gone out."
"4408","From al-Thawri, from Malik ibn Mighwal, from al-Sha'bi, he said: One prays on the ship standing."
"4409","From al-Thawri, from 'Asim, from Ibn Sirin, that Masruq used to carry a brick with him on the ship to prostrate upon it."
"4410","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: One prays voluntary prayers on the ship, and turns to the qiblah when it turns."
"4411","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: 'Ata was asked about a man who comes out of the sea naked. He said: He prays sitting."
"4412","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Others said: If one of them leads them, their imam should stand in the prayer in the middle of the row, and let them make one row, if they wish standing and if they wish sitting, and let some of them lower their gaze from others."
"4413","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: When a man comes out of the sea naked, he prays sitting."
"4414","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: When people come out of the sea naked and one of them leads them, they pray sitting, with their imam among them in the row, gesturing with gestures. Ma'mar said: And if one of them has a garment, he leads them standing, standing in the row while they are behind him sitting, in a single row."
"4415","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: One who prays on a ship, and one who prays naked, prays sitting."
"4416","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Ishaq ibn 'Abd Allah, from Maymun ibn Mihran, he said: 'Ali was asked about the prayer of a naked person. He said: If he is where people see him, he prays sitting, and if he is where people do not see him, he prays standing."
"4417","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to 'Ata: Is the witr obligatory, or the two rak'ahs before dawn, or anything of prayer before the prescribed prayer or after it? He said: No."
"4418","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn Yusuf, Salih ibn Kaysan, and Muhammad ibn Isma'il informed me, from Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Witr is a right, but it is not like Maghrib."
"4419","From Ma'mar, and al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Asim ibn Damrah, from 'Ali, he said: Witr is not imperative like the prescribed prayer, but it is a sunnah which the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, established."
"4420","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: A man asked Ibn al-Musayyab about witr. He said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, performed witr, and if you leave it there is nothing upon you. Pray the forenoon prayer, and if you leave it there is nothing upon you. Pray two rak'ahs before Zuhr and two after it, and if you leave them there is nothing upon you. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sacrificed, and if you leave it there is nothing upon you. He said: I said: O Abu Muhammad, all this we knew except witr. He said: It reached me that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Allah is One and loves the odd-numbered."
"4421","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from 'Amr ibn Murrah al-Jamali, from Abu 'Ubaydah, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Perform witr, O people of the Qur'an, for Allah is One and loves the odd-numbered. A Bedouin said: What is the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying? The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: It is not for you nor your companions."
"4422","From 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad, from Qatadah, from Anas, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: I was commanded with witr and sacrifices, but they were not made binding upon me."
"4423","From Ma'mar, from Aban, from 'Ikrimah, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Three things are obligatory upon me and voluntary for you: sacrifice, forenoon prayer, and witr."
"4424","From Ma'mar, from 'Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from 'Ikrimah, he said: Ubayy ibn Ka'b asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about witr. He said: Witr is upon the people of the Qur'an."
"4425","From Ma'mar, or Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, he said: Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban narrated to us, from Ibn Muhayriz al-Jumahi of the people of Syria, from al-Mukhaddaji, he said: It was said to 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit, or I said to him: Abu Muhammad says witr is obligatory. 'Ubadah said: Abu Muhammad lied. I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: Five prayers Allah has prescribed for the servants; whoever brings them without diminishing anything from them out of contempt for their right, it is binding upon Allah to admit him to Paradise, and whoever does not bring them has no covenant with Allah: if He wills He forgives him, and if He wills He punishes him."
"4426","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, he said: I said to Ibrahim: About a girl of six or five years, do you command her with witr? He said: Two rak'ahs after 'Isha; it used to be said witr is upon the people of the Qur'an."
"4427","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from 'Ammar al-Duhni, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, he said: Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman said: There is no witr except upon one who recites the Qur'an."
"4428","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, he said: An informant informed me, from Ibn 'Umar, he said: I would not like to have left witr for a single night, even if I had red camels."
"4429","From Ma'mar, from al-Hasan, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Allah is One and loves the odd-numbered, so whoever does not perform witr is not of us."
"4430","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Abu Hurayrah used to say: Allah is One and loves the odd-numbered."
"4431","Ayyub said, or another: Ibn Sirin used to like the odd number in everything, so much so that he would eat an odd number."
"4432","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: 'Abd al-Karim narrated to me that 'Ali used to affirm the witr."
"4433","From al-Muthanna, he said: 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb informed me, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came out to his Companions and said: Allah has added a prayer to your prayers, so guard it, and it is witr. And Ibn Jurayj mentioned it, from al-Muthanna, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb."
"4434","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Mujahid: Witr is incumbent but not prescribed in writing. And he said it from Ibn Jurayj, from Mujahid."
"4435","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he used to deem witr obligatory, and would say: Whoever misses witr until morning, let him perform witr when he remembers."
"4436","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Tawus, he said: Witr is made up."
"4437","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from him, he said: Witr is obligatory, to be repeated if forgotten."
"4438","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: Pray witr even if you have prayed dawn. Al-Thawri said: Whoever forgets 'Isha and prays witr after the twilight has vanished, he said: He prays 'Isha when he remembers it, and does not repeat witr."
"4439","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Perform witr before you enter morning."
"4440","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, he said: I heard Sa'id ibn Jubayr asked about a man who did not perform witr until morning. He said: He will perform witr some other day."
"4441","From Ja'far, from Sulayman, from Abu Harun al-'Abdi, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: I do not know except that he raised it, he said: Whoever is overtaken by dawn without having performed witr, there is no witr for him."
"4442","From Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata, he was asked about a man who did not perform witr until dawn broke. He said: Witr has passed him, so he should not perform witr. It was said to him: Is it knowledge or opinion?"
"4443","Then Humayd narrated, from Sulayman or Mina, from Ibn 'Umar, he said: They are only two rak'ahs; when dawn rises there is no prayer except two rak'ahs."
"4444","Then he informed me after that, that Ibn 'Abbas said to a servant of his: Look, has dawn brightened? He returned to him and said: The people are in prayer. So Ibn 'Abbas stood, performed witr with one rak'ah, then bowed two rak'ahs before dawn, and the hadith of Qatadah from Ibn 'Abbas about neglecting prayers."
"4445","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, he said: When you have prayed dawn, there is no witr."
"4446","From 'Abd Allah ibn Muharrar, from Qatadah, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: Perform witr as long as the sun has not risen."
"4447","From Ma'mar, from al-Hasan and Qatadah, they said: There is no witr after the dawn prayer."
"4448","From Ma'mar, from 'Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from 'Ata, that Ibn 'Abbas performed witr after the rising of dawn."
"4449","From Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: Pray witr even if you have prayed dawn. From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, the like of it."
"4450","From al-Thawri, from Ash'ath, and Ibn 'Awn, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Perform witr even at midday if you forgot."
"4451","And al-Thawri mentioned, from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi al-Safar, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Witr is the noblest voluntary prayer; it is not proper to abandon it, nor is it made up."
"4452","From Ma'mar, from Hammad, he said: Perform witr even if the sun has risen."
"4453","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Asim ibn Damrah, he said: A group came to Abu Musa al-Ash'ari and asked him about witr. He said: There is no witr after the call to prayer. So they went to 'Ali and informed him, and he said: He has drawn the bow to its fullest and exceeded in giving fatwa; witr is between you and the morning prayer."
"4454","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Asim ibn Damrah, he said: A man came to 'Ali and said: Abu Musa says there is no witr after the call to prayer. 'Ali said to him: He has drawn the bow to its fullest and exceeded in giving fatwa; witr is between the two prayers."
"4455","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed, from Abu al-Darda, he said: There is no witr for one overtaken by dawn. That was mentioned to 'A'ishah, and she said: Abu al-Darda lied; the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would enter morning and then perform witr."
"4456","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, that Ibn Mas'ud said: Witr is between the two prayers."
"4457","From Ma'mar, and al-Thawri, from Ash'ath ibn Abi al-Sha'tha and Abu Husayn, from al-Aswad ibn Hilal, he said: 'Abd Allah said: Witr is between the two prayers. From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad ibn Hilal, from 'Abd Allah, the like of that."
"4458","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Khalid ibn Abi Karimah, he said: I heard Mu'awiyah ibn Qurrah saying: A man came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: I did not perform witr until morning. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Witr is only by night. He repeated to him, so he ordered him to perform witr."
"4459","From al-Thawri, from Adam ibn 'Ali, he said: I heard Ibn 'Umar saying: Whoever enters morning without witr enters morning with a rope on his head seventy cubits long."
"4460","From Ma'mar, from a man, from Abu Nadrah, he said: Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas was once detained from prayer, and it was said to him: You delayed the people. He said to him: Morning overtook me before I performed witr, so I performed witr."
"4461","From 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, from 'Asim ibn 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Asim, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amir ibn Rabi'ah, he said: He would sometimes perform witr while hearing the iqamah."
"4462","From al-Thawri, from al-Zubayr ibn 'Adi, from Ibrahim, he said: I asked 'Abidah about a man who wakes at the iqamah without having performed witr. He said: He performs witr."
"4463","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', that a man asked Ibn 'Umar about witr. He said: While Ibn 'Umar was circumambulating the House one night, morning suddenly overtook him, so he performed witr."
"4464","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: When dawn rises, all night prayer and witr have gone, so perform witr before dawn."
"4465","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from Tamim ibn Salamah, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to pray at night, and when he finished he would say to me: Stand and perform witr."
"4466","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, from Ibn al-Musayyab, that Abu Bakr and 'Umar discussed witr before the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Abu Bakr said: As for me, I sleep upon witr, and if I wake I pray even-numbered until morning. 'Umar said: But I sleep upon even, then perform witr before dawn. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to Abu Bakr: This one was cautious. And he said to 'Umar: This one was strong."
"4467","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that Abu Bakr used to perform witr at the beginning of the night, and 'Umar at the end of the night. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, asked them about their witr, and they informed him. He said: This one was strong, and this one was cautious. He said: And the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: I strike for you the example of two men who set out in a desert at night; one said: I do not wish to sleep until I cross it, and the other said: I sleep a nap, then rise and cross it. Both reached the destination in the morning."
"4468","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn Yusuf informed me, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to Abu Bakr: When do you perform witr? He said: Before I sleep. He said: You have taken hold of the firmest handhold. And he said to 'Umar: When do you perform witr? He said: At the end of the night when I finish my prayer. He said: You did the deed of the strong."
"4469","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, advised me with three things I will not abandon in residence or travel: sleeping upon witr, fasting three days of each month, and the two rak'ahs of forenoon. He said: Then al-Hasan later became confused, and put in place of the two rak'ahs of forenoon: washing on Friday."
"4470","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, that Abu Bakr used to perform witr at the beginning of the night, saying: How hot are my wide-ranging supererogatory prayers."
"4471","From Ma'mar, from Abu 'Amr al-Nadabi, he said: I heard Rafi' ibn Khadij asked about witr. He said: As for me, I perform witr at the beginning of the night, and if I am granted something at its end, I pray two by two until morning. Or he said: Until morning overtakes me."
"4472","From Ja'far ibn Sulayman, from Malik ibn Dinar, he said: Khilas ibn 'Amr narrated to us, he said: I was sitting with 'Ammar ibn Yasir when a man asked him: O Abu al-Yaqzan, what do you say about witr? 'Ammar said: As for me, I perform witr before I sleep, and if Allah grants me something, I pray even-numbered, two by two, until dawn."
"4473","From Malik, and Ibn Zayd ibn Aslam, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abu Murrah, freedman of 'Aqil, he said: I asked Abu Hurayrah: Tell me how the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to perform witr? He fell silent. Then I asked him a second time, and he fell silent. Then I asked him a third time, and he said: If you wish I will tell you about Abu Hurayrah. As for me, I perform it here with five, then return and sleep, and if I wake I pray even-numbered until morning."
"4474","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: When one of you prostrates, let him be balanced, and not spread his forearms as a dog spreads them."
"4475","He said: And the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever among you fears he will not wake at the end of the night, let him perform witr at the beginning of the night, and whoever among you hopes to wake at the end of the night, let him perform witr at the end of the night, for the recitation at the end of the night is attended, and that is better."
"4476","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, from 'A'ishah, she said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, performed witr in every part of the night, its beginning, its middle, and its end, and his witr ended at the time before dawn."
"4477","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from 'Ali, that he used to perform witr at the call to prayer."
"4478","And al-Hasan ibn 'Umarah mentioned it, from Isra'il, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from 'Ali, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: That he used to perform witr at the call to prayer."
"4479","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from 'Alqamah, he said: I asked him, and he used to spend the night with 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, when did 'Abd Allah perform witr? He said: He used to perform witr when there remained of the night as much as had passed of the night when he prayed Maghrib. He said: And the people of the house would hear 'Abd Allah's recitation at night."
"4480","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, he said: I asked 'A'ishah: When do you perform witr? She said: Between the call to prayer and the iqamah. He said: And they would not call until morning."
"4481","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: He was asked about witr, and said: The witr of the clever is at the beginning of the night, and the witr of the strong is at the end of the night. I said: What do you do? He said: As for me, if I can be among the clever, I am."
"4482","From al-Thawri, from 'Asim ibn Abi al-Najud, from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, he said: 'Ali came out when Ibn al-Nabbah called the second call, and said: By the night when it departs and the morning when it breathes, what a good hour of witr this is; where are those who ask about witr?"
"4483","From al-Hasan ibn 'Umarah, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Abd Khayr, he said: 'Ali came out to us when dawn rose, and said: By the night when it departs, gesturing with his hand toward the east, then said: Where are those who ask about witr? What a good hour of witr this is."
"4484","From Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, he said: Ibn Mas'ud used to perform witr after dawn. He said: And my father used to perform witr before dawn."
"4485","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ata ibn Yazid al-Laythi, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, he said: Witr is a right upon every Muslim; whoever wishes to perform witr with five rak'ahs, let him do so, and whoever wishes to perform witr with three, let him do so, and whoever wishes to perform witr with one, let him do so, and whoever can only gesture with gestures, let him do so. From one who heard Anas narrating the like of that."
"4486","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Malik ibn al-Harith, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: The witr of the night is like the witr of the day, the Maghrib prayer, three. The saying of Ibn Mas'ud."
"4487","From Ma'mar, from Thabit al-Bunani, he said: I prayed with Anas and spent the night with him. He said: I saw him praying two by two, until when he was at the end of his prayer he performed witr with three like Maghrib."
"4488","From al-Thawri, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abd Allah, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu 'Ubaydah, he said: 'Abd Allah used to perform witr with three and more."
"4489","From Ma'mar, and al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: Witr is three, five, seven, nine, and eleven."
"4490","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Isma'il ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas informed me, that Sa'id ibn 'Ubayd ibn al-Sabbaq al-Thaqafi informed him, that when 'Umar buried Abu Bakr and finished, having prayed the last 'Isha prayer, he performed witr with three rak'ahs, and people of the Muslims performed witr with him."
"4491","From Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata, he said: Three rak'ahs are more beloved to me to perform witr with than a single rak'ah."
"4492","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: 'Utbah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Harith informed me, that 'Ikrimah, freedman of Ibn 'Abbas, informed him, he said: Ibn 'Abbas came as a delegate to Mu'awiyah in Syria, and they would converse until half the night or more. He said: Ibn 'Abbas attended the last 'Isha with Mu'awiyah one night in the enclosure, and when Mu'awiyah finished he bowed one rak'ah and added nothing to it, while I looked at him. He said: I went to Ibn 'Abbas and said to him: Shall I not laugh at Mu'awiyah? He prayed 'Isha, then performed witr with one rak'ah, adding nothing to it. He said: He was right, O my son; none of us is more knowledgeable than Mu'awiyah. It is only one, or five, or seven, or more than that; one performs witr with whatever he wishes. So I informed 'Ata of this report of 'Utbah, and he said: We only heard that he said: He was right. Is not Maghrib three rak'ahs,' 'Ata saying."
"4493","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: 'Ata was asked about a single rak'ah in which one performs witr. He said: Good; it reached me that Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas used to perform witr with one rak'ah."
"4494","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Hafs ibn 'Umar ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas informed me, he said: Sa'd used to pray 'Isha, then perform witr with a single rak'ah."
"4495","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas used to pray one rak'ah after it, then perform witr with it, then sleep until he would rise in the middle of the night. Ma'mar said: And I prayed 'Isha with Ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas; when he finished the prescribed prayer he stood and prayed one rak'ah. I said when he finished: Did you err in your prayer? He said: No. I said: You prayed one rak'ah. He said: We, the people of the household, do that."
"4496","From al-Thawri, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami: that Sad used to perform Witr with one rakah."
"4497","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yazid ibn Khusayfah, who said: I heard Muhammad ibn Shurahbil say: I saw Sad ibn Malik pray Isha, then pray one rakah after it with which he performed Witr."
"4498","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ismail ibn Muhammad ibn Sad, who said: I heard Musab ibn Sad ibn Abi Waqqas say to Sad: you perform Witr with a single rakah. He said: Yes, I make it light upon myself; three is dearer to me than one, five is dearer to me than three, and seven is dearer to me than five."
"4499","From Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Amash, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, who said: Witr is seven or five; three is butayra (docked short), and I dislike its being butayra."
"4500","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: three is dearer to me than one, seven is dearer to me than five, and the more the dearer to me."
"4501","From al-Thawri, who said: Witr is one rakah, three, five, seven, nine, and eleven; the most pleasing of them to me is three."
"4502","From a man, from al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, who said: Abdullah ibn Masud said to Sad ibn Abi Waqqas: do you perform Witr with one? He said: is Witr not but one? Abdullah said: yes, but three is better. He said: I will not add to it. He said: so Abdullah became angry, and Sad said: are you angry with me for performing Witr with one rakah, while you give inheritance to three grandmothers - will you then not give inheritance to Hawwa, the wife of Adam? Yahya informed me of it, from al-Thawri."
"4503","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid, who said: I saw Muawiyah pray Isha, then perform Witr after it with one rakah; I mentioned that to Ibn Abbas, and he said: he did right."
"4504","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Yazid ibn Khusayfah informed me, from al-Saib ibn Yazid, that a man asked Abd al-Rahman ibn Uthman al-Taymi about the prayer of Talhah ibn Ubaydullah. He said: if you wish, I will tell you about the prayer of Uthman ibn Affan. He said: yes. He said: I said to myself: I shall surely outdo the company tonight at the Hijr, meaning the Station. He said: when I stood, a veiled man crowded me; I looked, and it was Uthman, so I stepped back from him. He prayed, performing the prostrations of the Quran, until when I said: this is the call of Fajr, he performed Witr with one rakah and prayed nothing besides it, then left."
"4505","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yazid ibn Khusayfah, from al-Saib ibn Yazid, from a man of Quraysh, who said: I was praying behind the Maqam when a veiled man came and recited the seven long surahs, then bowed two rakahs, then turned away; I looked, and it was Uthman."
"4506","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: a man said: I saw Uthman one night praying, until when it was the end of the night he performed Witr; I followed him to see who he was, and it was Uthman."
"4507","From Abdullah, from one who heard him, from al-Hakam, who said: I said to Miqsam: I perform Witr with three, then go out to Fajr fearing the prayer will escape me; he disliked that one perform Witr with less than five or seven. I said: from whom is this? He said: from a trustworthy one, from Maymunah and Aishah, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"4508","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: a man asked Ata about the least that suffices the traveler. He said: one rakah if he wishes. He said: I said: and the resident? He said: one rakah suffices him too if he wishes, adding nothing to them."
"4509","From Ismail ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Awn, from Ibn Sirin, who said: Abdullah ibn Masud and Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman sat talking at night with al-Walid ibn Uqbah ibn Abi Muayt, then left him and stood conversing until they saw the first light of dawn; each of them performed Witr with one rakah."
"4510","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, who said: Ubayy ibn Kab used to perform Witr with three, giving salam only in the third, like Maghrib; from Hisham, from al-Hasan, from his father, the like of it."
"4511","From Ibn Jurayj, from Imran ibn Musa, from Yazid ibn Khusayfah, from al-Saib ibn Yazid: that Ubayy ibn Kab used to perform Witr with three."
"4512","From Mamar, from Thabit, from Anas: that he performed Witr with three."
"4513","From Mamar, from Thabit, from Anas: that he performed Witr with three like Maghrib."
"4514","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: do I separate between Witr and what precedes it with a salam? He said: as if you are bedouins; do you not give the salam of parting? Everything suffices you; if you wish pray a hundred rakahs, or do not separate between Witr and the bowings before it. He said: I said: and the imam likewise in Ramadan? He said: yes."
"4515","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Muhammad ibn Yusuf informed me, from Urwah, that he performed Witr with five, sitting only in the Witr one."
"4516","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata informed me that he saw Urwah ibn al-Zubayr perform Witr with five or seven, not sitting for the even pairs."
"4517","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Aishah: that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to perform Witr with five, not sitting between them."
"4518","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Umm Salamah, who said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to perform Witr with five or seven, not separating between them with speech or salam."
"4519","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: that he used to perform Witr with three, not sitting between them."
"4520","From Mamar, from Qatadah: that Ibn Umar used to attend to his need in two rakahs before Witr."
"4521","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, from Ata, who said: Ibn Abbas said: Witr is like the Maghrib prayer, except that one sits only in the third."
"4522","From Abdullah ibn Muharrar, from Qatadah: that Abu Musa al-Ashari, Abu Hurayrah, and Ibn Umar used to give salam between the two rakahs and the Witr."
"4523","Ibn Jurayj informed us, saying: Nafi informed me, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: whoever stands at night, let him make the last of it Witr before Fajr."
"4524","From Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, who said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was asked about the night prayer, and he said: two by two; when you fear Fajr, perform Witr with one, making odd what preceded it."
"4525","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ibn Sirin, from Ibn Umar, who said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: the night prayer is two by two, and Witr is one rakah at the end of the night. He said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Maghrib is the Witr of the daytime prayer, so perform Witr for the night prayer. Hisham said, and Ibn Sirin said: I have not seen anyone learned from hold except that Witr at the end of the night is better for whoever is able."
"4526","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Ibn Umar, who said: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: the night prayer is two by two, and Witr is one rakah at the end of the night. He said: and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: the Maghrib prayer is the Witr of the day, so perform Witr for the night prayer."
"4527","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from Salim ibn Abdullah, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman: that a man asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about the night prayer, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: the night prayer is two by two; when you fear Fajr, perform Witr with one."
"4528","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, who said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: the night prayer is two by two; when you fear Fajr, perform Witr with one."
"4529","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Tawus, from Ibn Umar, who said: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was asked about the night prayer, and he said: two by two; when you fear Fajr, then one."
"4530","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, who said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was asked about the night prayer, and he said: one of you prays two by two, until when he fears Fajr he performs Witr with one, making odd what he has prayed."
"4531","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, who said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: the night prayer is two by two; when you fear Fajr, perform Witr with one."
"4532","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar: that when he slept having performed Witr, then stood to pray at night, he would pray one rakah added to his Witr, making it even, then perform Witr again at the end of his prayer. Al-Zuhri said: that reached Ibn Abbas and did not please him, and he said: Ibn Umar performs Witr three times in a night."
"4533","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: there is no harm in a man performing Witr then sleeping; when he stands at night, he adds one rakah to his Witr making it even, then performs Witr at the end of his prayer. He said: and al-Hasan disliked that."
"4534","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Harun al-Abdi, from Hittan al-Raqashi, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, who said: if you wish, when you have performed Witr, stand and make it even with a rakah, then perform Witr after that; if you wish, pray two rakahs after Witr; and if you wish, delay Witr until you perform Witr at the end of the night."
"4535","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: I heard Ibn Abbas say: when one performs Witr at the beginning of the night, let him not make it even with a rakah, but pray in pairs until morning. He said: and Ata used to give fatwa, saying: when one performs Witr at the beginning of the night, then wakes after, let him pray in pairs until morning."
"4536","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, who said: when you have performed Witr at the beginning of the night, pray in pairs until morning."
"4537","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Umarah, from Abu Atiyyah, from Aishah, who said: there was mentioned to her the man who performs Witr then wakes and makes it even with a rakah. She said: that is playing with his Witr."
"4538","He said: and I asked Aishah about turning in prayer, and she said: it is a snatching which Satan snatches from the prayer."
"4539","From al-Thawri, from al-Zubayr ibn Adi, from Ibrahim, who said: I said to him: the man performs Witr at night, then wakes while night remains. He said: good; and they used to like their last prayer to be Witr."
"4540","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: when he performed Witr at night, he would pray in pairs until morning."
"4541","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Tawus, when he performed Witr at night, would not make it even, but pray in pairs until morning."
"4542","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Tawus, when he performed Witr at night, would not make it even; sometimes he would perform Witr at its beginning once and at its end another time. He mentioned it from his father."
"4543","From al-Thawri, who said: Ibn Qays al-Awdi informed me, saying: I asked Amr ibn Maymun al-Awdi about undoing Witr. He said: when you have performed Witr, then stand at night, make it even with a rakah. He said: I mentioned that to Alqamah, and he said: Amr does not know; Witr is but one, so when you have performed Witr, then wake at night, pray in pairs until morning."
"4544","From Mamar, from a shaykh, from Masruq, that he said: when one sleeps having performed Witr, then wakes, he prays in pairs until morning; and the hadith of Ammar, Rafi ibn Khadij, Abu Hurayrah, and Abu Bakr is like this."
"4545","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Mijlaz, from Qays ibn Abbad, who said: when he had performed Witr while night remained, he would sit reciting until morning."
"4546","Mamar informed us, from Qatadah, from Said ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father: that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to perform Witr with Glorify the name of your Lord the Most High, and Say O disbelievers, and Say He is Allah the One."
"4547","From al-Thawri, from Zubayd al-Yami, from Dharr ibn Abdillah al-Murhibi, from Said ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father, who said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to perform Witr with Glorify the name of your Lord the Most High, and Say O disbelievers, and Say He is Allah the One; when he wanted to finish Witr he would say: Glory be to Allah the King the Holy, three times, then raise his voice in the third. From Amr ibn Dharr, from his father, from Said ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"4548","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I was told, from Aishah, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to recite in the last three rakahs: in the first, Glorify the name of your Lord the Most High; in the second, Say O disbelievers; and in the third, Say He is Allah the One, and Say I seek refuge with the Lord of the daybreak, and Say I seek refuge with the Lord of mankind."
"4549","From al-Thawri, from Salm ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Zadham, from Ali: that he used to perform Witr with Indeed We sent it down in the Night of Decree, and When the earth is shaken, and Say He is Allah the One."
"4550","From al-Thawri, from Mansur and others, from Ibrahim: that he liked to recite in the last rakah of Witr: Say He is Allah the One, and The Messenger believes in what was sent down to him from his Lord."
"4551","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: recite in them whatever you wish; nothing is fixed in them."
"4552","From al-Thawri, from Mansur and others, from Ibrahim, who said: say takbir when you finish recitation in the last rakah of Witr, then stand in qunut raising your voice; then when you wish to bow, say takbir."
"4553","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata told me: it reached me that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to perform Witr with thirteen rakahs, among them two rakahs before Fajr. I said: how did he pray them? He said: I do not know."
"4554","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, who said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to pray eleven rakahs at night; when dawn broke he would pray two light rakahs, then recline on his right side until the muadhdhin came to inform him of the prayer."
"4555","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to me, from a freedman of the Ansar, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, who said: Muadh ibn Jabal said: who will go ahead and draw water for us? He said: I said: I - and that was on their return from Hudaybiyah. Jabir said: I came to a hollow holding water, drew water and filled the basin; the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, arrived and said: will you give drink? I said: yes, may my father be your ransom. He drank, then I took his halter or rein and led him to an open plain where he alighted; he prayed thirteen rakahs, I with him at his side, after the latter Isha. He said: I think he said: he prayed the latter Isha then prayed them."
"4556","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, from Ibn Abbas, who said: I was in the house of Maymunah; the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stood praying at night; I stood with him on his left, so he took my hand and placed me on his right; then he prayed thirteen rakahs, until I estimated his standing in each rakah as much as O you wrapped up."
"4557","Al-Thawri informed us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas, who said: I slept at my aunt Maymunah bint al-Harith; the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stood at night, relieved himself, then came and washed his face and hands, then slept; then he stood at night, went to the waterskin, loosened its string, performed wudu between two wudus, not excessive yet thorough; then he stood praying. I stretched, disliking that he see I was seeking him, meaning watching him. He said: then I stood and did as he did; I stood on his left, so he took what is by my ear until he turned me to his right. His prayer completed to thirteen rakahs, among them the two of Fajr; then he lay down and slept until he snored; then Bilal came and informed him of the prayer; he stood and prayed without performing wudu. Sufyan said: it was mentioned to us from Ibn Abbas that it was mentioned to him, and he said: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was protected. He said: and some jurists said: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - his eye sleeps, his heart does not sleep."
"4558","Yahya added to me, from al-Thawri: Ibn Abbas said: in his supplication he would say: O Allah, place light in my heart, light in my hearing, light in my tongue, light in my sight, light on my left, light above me, light beneath me, light before me, light behind me, and magnify light for me. Kurayb said: and six are with me in the chest: my sinew, my marrow, my blood, my hair, my skin, and my bones."
"4559","Malik informed us, from Makhramah ibn Sulayman, from Kurayb, that Ibn Abbas told him he spent the night at his aunt Maymunah. He said: I lay across the pillow, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and his wife lay along it. He stayed till midnight, or a little before or after; then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, woke, sat, wiped sleep from his face, then recited the ten closing verses of Surah Al Imran; then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, went to a hanging skin, performed wudu well, then stood and prayed. I stood and did like he did, then went to his side; he placed his hand on my head and took my ear twisting it. He prayed two, then two, then two, then performed Witr; then he lay down till the muadhdhin came; then he stood and prayed two light rakahs, then went out and prayed Fajr."
"4560","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abdullah ibn Abi Mulaykah, who said: Yala ibn Mamlak told me he asked Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about the prayer of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, at night. She said: he would pray the latter Isha, then glorify Allah, then pray after it what he willed of the night, then leave and sleep like what he prayed, then wake from that sleep and pray like what he slept; that last prayer of his would extend to Fajr."
"4561","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would pray seventeen rakahs at night."
"4562","From Malik, from Said ibn Abi Said, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, that he asked Aishah: how was the prayer of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in Ramadan? She said: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would not exceed in Ramadan nor otherwise eleven rakahs: he would pray four, ask not of their beauty and length; then pray four, ask not of their beauty and length; then pray three. Aishah said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, do you sleep before performing Witr? He said: O Aishah, my eyes sleep, my heart does not sleep."
"4563","Malik said, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Qays ibn Makhramah, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, that he said: I shall surely observe the prayer of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He said: I pillowed his threshold or tent; the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stood and prayed two light rakahs, then prayed two long ones, then prayed two shorter than the two before them, then performed Witr: that is thirteen rakahs."
"4564","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, who said: Sad ibn Hisham told me he heard Aishah say: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to perform Witr with nine rakahs, two while sitting; when he grew weak he performed Witr with seven, two while sitting."
"4565","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa: that Sad ibn Hisham ibn Amir was his neighbor and told him he divorced his wife, then set out to Madinah to sell his property and wealth to put it into arms and horses for those fighting the Romans until death. People of his tribe met him, forbade him that, and told him that six of them intended that at the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade them, saying to them: is there not in me an example for you? When they told him that, he took back his wife."
"4566","When he came to us he was told he went to Ibn Abbas and asked him about Witr. Ibn Abbas said: shall I not tell you, or guide you to, the most knowledgeable of the people of the earth about the Witr of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him? I said: who? He said: Aishah; go to her and ask her about that, then return to me and tell me her reply to you. Sad ibn Hisham said: I went to Hakim ibn Aflah and asked him to accompany me to her. He said: I will not go near her; I forbade her to say anything between the two parties, but she insisted on going on in it. I swore upon him, so he came with me; we greeted her; he entered and she recognized him, saying: Hakim? He said: yes. She said: who is this with you? He said: Sad ibn Hisham. She said: which Hisham? He said: ibn Amir. She said: what an excellent man Amir was; he was struck with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, on the day of Uhud. He said: I said: O Mother of the Believers, tell me of the character of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. She said: do you not recite the Quran? I said: yes. She said: the character of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was the Quran."
"4567","He said: I intended to stand, then it occurred to me, and I said to her: tell me of the standing of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. She said: do you not recite this surah, O you wrapped up? He said: I said: yes. She said: Allah made standing obligatory at the beginning of this surah; the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and his Companions stood for about a year, till their feet swelled, and Allah withheld its ending twelve months; then Allah sent down the lightening at the end of the surah, so night standing became voluntary after being obligatory. I intended to stand, then it occurred to me, so I asked her, saying: O Mother of the Believers, tell me of the Witr of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. She said: we would prepare for him his siwak and purification at night; Allah would raise him as He willed to raise him; then he would use siwak, perform wudu, then pray nine rakahs, sitting in them only at the eighth, praising Allah, remembering Him, supplicating Him; then he would rise without giving salam till he prayed the ninth; he would sit, praise Allah, remember Him, supplicate Him, then give a salam we could hear; then he would pray two rakahs sitting after giving salam: that is eleven rakahs, O my son. When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, aged and flesh took him, he performed Witr with seven, praying two rakahs sitting after giving salam: that is nine, O my son. The Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when he prayed a prayer, liked to persist in it; and the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when sleep or pain overcame him from night standing, would pray twelve rakahs by day. I do not know the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, to have recited the whole Quran in a night, nor stood a night till morning, nor stood a month besides Ramadan. He said: I went to Ibn Abbas and told him her hadith; he said: you spoke true; had I entered upon her I would have spoken to her of it face to face. Hakim ibn Aflah said: had I known you would not enter upon her, I would not have told you her hadith."
"4568","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Umarah, from Yahya ibn al-Jazzar, from Aishah, who said: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to pray nine at night; when he grew heavy and aged he prayed seven."
"4569","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: I said to him: do we restrict to the Witr of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him? He said: rather, increase of good is dearer to me."
"4570","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: they liked after Witr a lying down or a nap."
"4571","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu al-Nadr or Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Aishah, who said: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would pray at night; when he wanted to perform Witr, if I was awake he would talk to me, otherwise he would lie down."
"4572","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: that Abu Musa al-Ashari, Rafi ibn Khadij, and Anas ibn Malik would lie down at the two rakahs of Fajr and enjoin that."
"4573","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, who said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when dawn rose, would pray two light rakahs, then recline on his right side till the muadhdhin came to inform him of the prayer."
"4574","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: one I trust told me that Aishah said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when dawn rose, would pray two light rakahs, then recline on his right side till the muadhdhin came to inform him of the prayer. He did not recline as a sunnah, but he would toil at night and rest. He said: Ibn Umar would pelt them when he saw them reclining on their right sides."
"4575","From Mamar and Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Shihab narrated to us, from Urwah, from Aishah, who said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, went out one night in the middle of the night and prayed in the mosque; men gathered and prayed with him his prayer. In the morning they talked that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, had gone out and prayed in the mosque in the middle of the night. The next night more of them gathered; the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, went out in the middle of the night, they gathered, he prayed and they prayed with him his prayer; in the morning they talked of that. Many people gathered to him the third night till the people of the mosque increased. She said: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, went out in the middle of the night, prayed, and they prayed with him. She said: when it was the fourth night, people gathered till the mosque nearly could not hold its people. She said: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sat and did not go out. She said: till I heard some of them saying: the prayer. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not go out to them. When he prayed Fajr he gave salam, then stood among the people, bore witness, then said: as for after: your affair tonight was not hidden from me, but I feared it would be made obligatory upon you and you would be unable for it."
"4576","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, who said: it reached me that They are not alike; among the People of the Book is an upright nation was revealed about between Maghrib and Isha."
"4577","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from his father al-Aswad ibn Yazid, who said: Abdullah said: excellent is the hour of heedlessness between Maghrib and Isha, meaning prayer."
"4578","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from al-Ala ibn Badr, from a man, from Salman, who said: pray between Maghrib and Isha, for it lightens one's portion and removes from him the idle talk of early night; the idle talk of early night is a lull for its end."
"4579","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ata, from Tubay, who said: whoever prays after Isha four rakahs, doing well in recitation, bowing, and prostration, shall have like the reward of Laylat al-Qadr."
"4580","From Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Ayyub ibn Khalid, from Ibn Umar - I know only he raised it - who said: whoever bows after Maghrib four rakahs is like one following one raid with another."
"4581","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, who said: al-Hasan ibn Ali saw a man praying after Maghrib four rakahs and said to him: did you miss anything of the prescribed? He said: no. He said: they are but two rakahs after the prostrations. And Mamar held this."
"4582","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, who said: tahajjud is but after sleep."
"4583","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, who said: Mujahid saw me pray after Maghrib and said: they are but two rakahs. Ibrahim said: I never saw Tawus add to two rakahs after Maghrib."
"4584","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, on His saying: Indeed the rising of the night is mightier: when one stands praying at night, it is nashiah."
"4585","Al-Thawri said, and Layth said, from Mujahid: whatever is after Isha is nashiah."
"4586","From Mamar, from Amr, from al-Hasan, who said: whatever is after Isha is nashiah."
"4587","From al-Numan ibn Abi Shaybah, from Salamah ibn Wahram and Abd al-Rahman ibn Budhawayh, that they heard Tawus say: whoever prays two rakahs before Fajr is among those seeking forgiveness in the pre-dawns."
"4588","From al-Thawri, from Zubayd, from Murrah, who said: Abdullah said: the excellence of night prayer over day prayer is like the excellence of secret charity over open charity. He said: and Abdullah said: you, as long as you are in prayer, are as knocking at the door of the King; whoever knocks at the door of the King is like to be opened for."
"4589","From a shaykh of the people of Madinah, from Ibn Shihab, who said: the excellence of night prayer over day prayer is like the excellence of the prescribed prayer over the voluntary."
"4590","Al-Thawri informed us, from his father, from al-Mughirah ibn Shubayl, from Tariq ibn Shihab, that he spent the night with Salman to see his striving. He said: he stood praying at the end of the night, as if he did not see what he expected. That was mentioned to him, so Salman said: guard these five prayers, for they are expiations for these wounds as long as the fatal blow is not struck. When people pray Isha they proceed in three stations: among them one against whom and not for him, among them one for whom and not against him, and among them one neither for him nor against him. As for the one against whom and not for him: a man seizing the darkness of the night and people's heedlessness, plunging his head into sins; that is against him and not for him. As for the one for whom and not against him: a man seizing the darkness of the night and people's heedlessness, standing to pray; that is for him and not against him. As for the one neither for him nor against him: a man who prayed and slept; that is neither for him nor against him. Beware of overexertion; upon you is moderation and constancy."
"4591","From al-Thawri, from Ali ibn al-Aqmar, from al-Agharr, from Abu Said al-Khudri, who said: when a man stands at night and wakes his wife and they pray two rakahs, they are written that night among the men remembering Allah much and the women remembering."
"4592","From al-Thawri, from Ibn al-Munkadir, who said: one who heard Abu Hurayrah narrated to me - I think only he raised it - saying: when one of you stands at night, let him wake his family; if she does not wake, let him sprinkle her face with water."
"4593","From Mamar, from a man of Quraysh and others, referring it to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, who said: Allah said: My most beloved servants to Me are those loving for the sake of religion, maintaining My mosques, seeking forgiveness in the pre-dawns; those whom, when I mention torment for My creation, I remember them and turn My torment from My creation."
"4594","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from a man, from Ali, on His saying: Protect yourselves: teach yourselves good."
"4595","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Umarah, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, who said: accustom yourselves to good, for good is but by habit."
"4596","From Malik, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father: that Umar would pray at night what Allah willed him to pray, until when it was the end of the night he would wake his family, saying: the prayer, the prayer, and recite this verse: Enjoin prayer upon your family and be constant in it; We ask you not for provision; We provide for you, and the end is for piety."
"4597","From Mamar, from a man of Quraysh, who said: when some straitness in provision befell the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he would enjoin his family to pray, then recite this verse: Enjoin prayer upon your family and be constant in it."
"4598","From Mamar, from Aban, mentioning from some of them, who said: when a man wakes at night, remembers Allah, stands, performs wudu, prays, and supplicates Allah, He answers him."
"4599","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from al-Mughirah ibn Shubah, who said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would pray till his feet cracked. It was said to him: has not Allah forgiven you what preceded of your sin and what followed? He said: shall I not be a thankful servant?"
"4600","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from some of his companions, who said: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would pray till his feet swelled. They said: O Messenger of Allah, you do this though your feet have swollen, and Allah has forgiven you what preceded of your sin and what followed? He said: shall I not be a thankful servant?"
"4601","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Abd al-Qari: that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: whoever sleeps past his portion - or he said: his part - of the night, then recites it between Fajr prayer and Zuhr prayer, it is as if he recited it at night."
"4602","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan: that a man saw Umar ibn al-Khattab praying at a time of day in which he did not used to pray, and said to him; he said: I missed it at night - and Allah has said: He who made night and day successive for whoever wishes to remember or wishes thanks."
"4603","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: they liked increase in deed, disliked decrease, and portions constantly; when something of the night escaped them they made it up by day."
"4604","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Aban ibn Abi Ayyash, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Aishah, who said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when he did not pray anything at night, would pray twelve rakahs by day."
"4605","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Mashrafi, who said: I heard al-Hasan say: when a man misses something of the night and does not pray, there is no harm in his prolonging the two rakahs of Fajr."
"4606","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I asked Ata: do you dislike prayer when dawn spreads over the mountain tops except the two rakahs of Fajr? He said: yes."
"4607","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Mina Abu Abd al-Rahman ibn Mina, or Sulaym, freedman of Said, told me - both, as far as I know, were praying men. He said: one of them told me, saying: I said: I came to the mosque after Fajr. He said: I kept praying in succession, so Ibn Umar said: what is this? He said: I said: I did not pray last night. Ibn Umar said: do you wish to tell me now! They are but two rakahs."
"4608","From al-Thawri, from Abu Riyah, from Ibn al-Musayyab: that he saw a man repeating bowing after dawn rise and forbade him. He said: O Abu Muhammad, will Allah torment me for prayer? He said: no, but He will torment you for opposing the Sunnah."
"4609","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah, from Ibn al-Musayyab, who said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: no prayer after the call except the two rakahs of Fajr."
"4610","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, from Abdullah ibn Amr, who said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: no prayer after dawn rise except the two rakahs of Fajr."
"4611","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Mujahid said: Ibn Abbas could not see, and it was seen for him; when dawn rose he would bow two rakahs then sit."
"4612","He said: and Ibn Umar would look; when dawn rose he would bow two rakahs then sit."
"4613","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, who said: Mujahid said to Tawus: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, I saw Ibn Abbas, after his sight went, ask his boy about Fajr; when he told him it had risen, he prayed two rakahs then sat."
"4614","And I saw Ibn Umar turn; when he saw Fajr he prayed two rakahs then sat. He said: Tawus said to him: do you understand? When dawn rises, pray what you wish."
"4615","From Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, who said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: no prayer after dawn rise except the two rakahs of Fajr."
"4616","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from al-Hasan, who said: pray after dawn rise what you wish."
"4617","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, who said: Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah told me, saying: I saw Tawus enter the mosque of Mina after dawn rose and pray eight rakahs; I asked him about that, and he said: a night prayer I used to pray, which I slept past."
"4618","He said: then I saw Ata after that enter the mosque of Mina after dawn rise and pray eight rakahs; I asked him, and he said like what Tawus said."
"4619","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Wabarah: that Ibn Umar repeated the two rakahs of Fajr in a night three times because he prayed them at night."
"4620","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: when was it liked that those two rakahs be bowed? He said: with Fajr or after it; separate between them and what you prayed before them."
"4621","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that he heard Ibn Abbas say: they are two dawns; as for the dawn shining in the sky, it is nothing and prohibits nothing; but the dawn spreading over the mountain tops is what prohibits. Ata said: when it shines shining in the sky, its shining going lengthwise in the sky, it does not prohibit drink for fasting, nor prayer, nor causes Hajj to be missed; but when it spreads over the mountain tops, drink becomes prohibited for fasting, and Hajj is missed."
"4622","Umar said: the dawn that is like the gold of the wolf - meaning: that shining in the sky."
"4623","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: what if I come to the mosque when dawn has spread - shall I lengthen them or shorten them? He said: lengthen them if you wish, as long as the imam has not come out."
"4624","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Abi Labid, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, who said: the two rakahs before Fajr prayer used to be lightened."
"4625","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Awf, from Ibn Sirin, who said: Witr is of the night; it is liked to be at the end of the night; takbir is liked at Fajr with the two rakahs, and they are of the daytime prayer."
"4626","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, who said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when dawn rose, would pray two light rakahs, then recline on his right side."
"4627","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, who said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would pray, when dawn rose, two light rakahs, then recline on his right side."
"4628","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, who said: Hafsah told me that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when dawn rose, would pray two light rakahs."
"4629","From Israil, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, who said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to pray the two rakahs of Fajr at the iqamah."
"4630","From Malik, from Muhammad ibn Makhramah ibn Sulayman, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas: that the muadhdhin came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he prayed two light rakahs, then went out and prayed Fajr."
"4631","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Said, from Amrah, from Aishah, who said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would lighten them - meaning the two rakahs of Fajr."
"4632","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali: that he used to bow them at the iqamah."
"4633","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: are the two rakahs of Duha, or Witr, or anything of voluntary before the prayers or after them, obligatory? He said: no."
"4634","From al-Thawri, from Hakim ibn Jubayr, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Aishah, who said: I never saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, hasten to anything as he hastened to the two rakahs before the morning prayer, nor to a booty he sought."
"4635","From Uthman ibn Matar, from Said, from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Aishah, who said: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: the two rakahs of Fajr are dearer to me than the world and what is in it."
"4636","From Abdullah ibn Muharrar, from Qatadah, from Anas, who said: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: the two rakahs of Fajr are dearer to me than the world and what is in it. He said: and Umar ibn al-Khattab said: they are dearer to me than red camels."
"4637","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Muhajir ibn al-Qutbiyyah, who said: Abdullah ibn Abi Rabiah missed the two rakahs of Fajr, so he freed a slave."
"4638","From Mamar, from Ayyub: Ibn Umar said to Humran: O Humran, fear Allah; do not die with a debt upon you, taken from your good deeds, no dinar there nor dirham; do not disown your child, disgracing him, lest Allah disgrace you with him on Resurrection Day; and upon you are the two rakahs of Fajr, for in them is the desire of the age."
"4639","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, who said: if you do not make up the two rakahs of Fajr, nothing is upon you - meaning: when they escape you."
"4640","From Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Awzai, from Urwah ibn Ruwaym, who said: whoever prays the two rakahs of Fajr, and prays Fajr in congregation, his prayer that day is written in the prayer of the oft-returning, and he is written that day among the delegation of the pious."
"4641","From Ath-Thawri, from Ziyad ibn Fayyad, from Abu Abd ar-Rahman as-Sulami, he said: I heard him saying: If a man prayed two rak'ahs before the morning prayer, then died, he would have performed the morning prayer."
"4642","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Al-Harith, from Ali, that he used to pray witr at the time of the adhan, and pray the two rak'ahs of Fajr at the iqamah."
"4643","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two rak'ahs of Fajr are dearer to me than the world and what is in it."
"4644","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: It was deemed desirable to recite in the two rak'ahs of Fajr: Say, O disbelievers, and Say, He is Allah, the One."
"4645","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited quietly in the two rak'ahs of Fajr, and he recited in them: Say, O disbelievers, and Say, He is Allah, the One. And Ath-Thawri mentioned it from Hisham."
"4646","Ath-Thawri informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) more than twenty-five times - or he said: more than twenty times, Abu Bakr doubted - reciting in the two rak'ahs of Fajr: Say, O disbelievers, and Say, He is Allah, the One."
"4647","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, that Ibn Abbas was asked - or Ibn Abbas was asked: What do you recite in the two rak'ahs of Fajr? He said: Say, O disbelievers, and Say, He is Allah, the One."
"4648","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from one who heard Amrah narrating, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray the two rak'ahs of Fajr, and I would say to you: Did he recite the Fatihah of the Book or not?"
"4649","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Amrah, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to stand for the two rak'ahs of Fajr, and I would say: Did he recite Umm al-Quran in them or not, because he prayed them so lightly?"
"4650","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Talking before the morning prayer is disliked. I said: Between the prayers? He said: Yes. I said: Why? He said: Do you not hear Him saying: And the recitation of dawn, indeed the recitation of dawn is witnessed - because it is witnessed and attended. I said: So one informs before Fajr? And he disliked that too."
"4651","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Ibn Mas'ud went out to some people who were talking, and he forbade them from talking, and said: You have come only for prayer; either pray or be silent."
"4652","From Yahya, from Ath-Thawri, and Ibn at-Taymi, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: Ibn Mas'ud passed by two men talking after the rising of Fajr, and said: You two, either pray or be silent."
"4653","From Ath-Thawri, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu Ubaydah ibn Abdullah, he said: It was grievous to Abdullah ibn Mas'ud to speak after the rising of Fajr except for the remembrance of Allah."
"4654","From Ath-Thawri and Ma'mar, from Al-A'mash, from Al-Qasim ibn Abd ar-Rahman, from his father, he said: Abdullah came at Fajr while they were leaning their backs against the qiblah, and said: Move away from the qiblah; do not come between the angels and the qiblah, for it is the prayer of the angels."
"4655","From Ma'mar, from Al-A'mash, from Al-Qasim ibn Abd ar-Rahman, from his father, he said: Ibn Mas'ud entered the mosque before the Fajr prayer and saw people who had leaned their backs against the qiblah and faced the people, so he said: Do not come between the angels and their prayer, for it is the prayer of the angels."
"4656","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, that Ibn Mas'ud used to dislike talking when he had prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr."
"4657","From Ma'mar and Ath-Thawri, from Khusayf, he said: I asked Sa'id ibn Jubayr about a verse after the two rak'ahs, and he did not answer me. Then when he prayed, he said: Talking after the two rak'ahs is disliked. I said: May a man tell his family to pray? He said: There is no harm."
"4658","From Ibn Jurayj, from Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman, he said: When Fajr rises, they should be silent, even if they are riders; and even if they have not prayed them, they should be silent. And it was mentioned that Ibn al-Musayyib used to say: Then I would be more foolish than the one who talks after Fajr has risen."
"4659","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I hear them mention two rak'ahs before Zuhr and after it, and two rak'ahs after Maghrib, and after Isha. He said: It has reached me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray thirteen rak'ahs after the last Isha, among them two rak'ahs before the morning prayer."
"4660","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ubayd ibn Umayr says: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: How many are the prayers? He said: Five, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named them for him. He said: And Ramadan. The questioner said: I will never add to them. Then he turned away, and they laughed at him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If he is truthful, he will enter Paradise. Ata said: If he establishes them, he will enter Paradise."
"4661","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is anything of the voluntary prayer obligatory? He said: No."
"4662","From Ma'mar and Ath-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, he said: We said to him: Narrate to us about the voluntary prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: And who can bear it? He said: We said to him: Narrate to us; we will bear of it what we can. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wait, then when the sun rose high, at about the measure of Asr time from the east, he prayed two rak'ahs, separating in them with salutations upon the close angels and the prophets and those who followed them among the believers and Muslims. Then he would wait until the forenoon rose high, at about its measure from Zuhr from the east, and pray four, separating in them with the salutation as he did in the first. Then when the sun declined, he stood and prayed four, separating in them with salutations upon the close angels and the prophets and those who followed them among the believers and Muslims. Then he would pray two rak'ahs after Zuhr like that. Then he would pray four before Asr and do likewise."
"4663","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, he said: We asked Ali ibn Abi Talib about the daytime voluntary prayer of the Messenger of Allah. He said: You cannot bear what he used to bear. They said: Nevertheless, narrate to us. He mentioned like the hadith of Ath-Thawri, except that he did not say: separating with salutations upon the close angels. He said: And he prays four before Zuhr and two after it, and four before Asr; these are sixteen rak'ahs."
"4664","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Ibn Shihab informed him, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray two rak'ahs before Zuhr and two after it, and two after Jumu'ah, and two after Maghrib, and two after Isha. And Ibn Umar was mentioned to me as saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray two rak'ahs before the morning prayer, though I did not see him."
"4665","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It reached me from Nafi', he said: Ibn Umar said: I prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) two rak'ahs before Zuhr and two after Zuhr, and two after Maghrib, and two after Isha, and two after Jumu'ah. As for Jumu'ah, Maghrib and Isha, they were in his house."
"4666","From Malik, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray two rak'ahs after Maghrib in his house, and he would not pray anything after Jumu'ah until he entered his house and prayed two rak'ahs in it."
"4667","Ma'mar informed us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: I memorized from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ten rak'ahs which he used to pray by night and day: two rak'ahs before Zuhr, two after it, two after Maghrib, and two after the last Isha."
"4668","And Hafsah narrated to me: That he used to pray two rak'ahs after the morning prayer. Ma'mar informed us, from Az-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, the like of it."
"4669","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ayyub ibn Abi Tamimah narrating, from Ibn Umar, he said: I prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) two rak'ahs before Zuhr and two after it, and two after Maghrib, and two after Isha. He said: And Hafsah said: And he used to pray two rak'ahs when the call was made, and no one would enter upon him at that time."
"4670","From Ath-Thawri, from Al-A'mash, from Al-Musayyab ibn Ayyub, from a man, from Abu Rafi' al-Ansari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray four before Zuhr. It was said to him: You pray a prayer regularly. He said: The doors of heaven are opened when the sun declines, and they are not closed until Zuhr is prayed, so I love that some good of mine ascends to heaven."
"4671","From Ath-Thawri, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu Ubaydah, he said: The regular voluntary prayer of Abdullah, from which he did not omit, was: four before Zuhr, two after it, two after Maghrib, two after Isha, and two before the morning prayer."
"4672","From Ma'mar, he said: It reached me that Ibn Mas'ud used to pray four rak'ahs before Zuhr and two after it."
"4673","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, from Abu Busrah, from Al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: I went on eighteen expeditions with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) omit two rak'ahs when the sun declined, neither in residence nor on journey."
"4674","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Abu Sufyan, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the shadows lengthen and the winds blow, mention your needs, for it is the hour of the oft-returning."
"4675","From Ath-Thawri, from Al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Sulayman ibn Mushir, from Kharashah, that Umar used to dislike praying right after a prescribed prayer one like it."
"4676","From Ma'mar, from Al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Sulayman ibn Mushir, from Kharashah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Do not pray right after every prescribed prayer one like it."
"4677","From Ath-Thawri, from Hammad, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: When you have given salutations, then it is not like it."
"4678","From Isra'il, from Thuwayr ibn Abi Fakhitah, from his father, that Ali used to pray two rak'ahs after Isha."
"4679","From Ath-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray two rak'ahs right after every prescribed prayer except Fajr and Asr."
"4680","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: I retained from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he used to pray two rak'ahs before Zuhr and two after it, and two after Jumu'ah, and two after Maghrib, and two after Isha."
"4681","He said: And Hafsah narrated to me: That when Fajr rose he prayed two light rak'ahs."
"4682","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from Shu'ayb ibn Khalid, from Abu Ishaq, from Abdullah ibn Budayl, he said: The most intimate of people with Abdullah ibn Mas'ud narrated to me that when the sun declined he stood and bowed four rak'ahs, reciting in them two surahs of the Mi'in, then when the mu'adhdhins answered one another he tightened his clothes upon him, then went out to prayer."
"4683","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Muhammad ibn Qays, from Awn ibn Abdullah, from his father, he said: I saw Umar ibn al-Khattab praying four in his house before Zuhr."
"4684","From Isra'il, from Majza'ah ibn Zahir, from Amr ibn al-Harith, the brother of Juwayriyah al-Khuza'i, he said: No prayer after the prescribed prayer is better than four rak'ahs before Zuhr."
"4685","From Isra'il, from Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Muhajir, from Anbasah ibn Abi Sufyan, from Umm Habibah: That she heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever prays four rak'ahs before Zuhr, Allah will forbid him to the Fire."
"4686","From Ath-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: The Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were not more assiduous about anything than about four rak'ahs before Zuhr and two rak'ahs before the morning prayer."
"4687","From Ath-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to count among the sunnah four before Zuhr and two after it. He said: And they used to bow two rak'ahs before Asr but did not count them among the sunnah, and two after Maghrib, and two after Isha, and two before Fajr."
"4688","From Ath-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: It was deemed desirable, when the four before Zuhr were missed, to pray those four after Zuhr."
"4689","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Al-Qasim ash-Shaybani, from Zayd ibn Arqam, that he saw people praying after the sun had risen, and said: Had these met the first predecessors, they would have known that another prayer is better than this: the prayer of the oft-returning, when the young camels feel the heat."
"4690","From Ath-Thawri, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, he said: I heard Makhul - and I had come to greet him - and he said: It reached me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays two rak'ahs after Maghrib before speaking, they are written - or raised - in Illiyyin."
"4691","From Ath-Thawri, from Ibrahim, from Abd ar-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: They used to deem it desirable to bow after Maghrib: Say, O disbelievers, and Say, He is Allah, the One; and after Isha in two rak'ahs with the end of Surah al-Baqarah, The Messenger has believed, and with Say, He is Allah, the One; and before Fajr with Say, O disbelievers, and Say, He is Allah, the One."
"4692","From Ath-Thawri, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Damrah ibn Habib ibn Suhayb, from a man among the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: A man's voluntary prayer in his house exceeds his voluntary prayer before people, like the excellence of the congregational prayer over a man's prayer alone. From Ma'mar, from Al-A'mash, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from a man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"4693","From Ath-Thawri, from Al-A'mash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: Abu Sa'id al-Khudri narrated to us, he said: When one of you prays a prayer in the mosque, let him give his house a share of his prayer, for Allah will place good in his house from his prayer."
"4694","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from Al-A'mash, from Muslim ibn Subayh, from Masruq, he said: We used to sit in the mosque after Abdullah ibn Mas'ud stood up, keeping the people firm upon recitation. Then when we wanted to return, we prayed two rak'ahs. That reached Abdullah, and he said: You burden the people with what Allah does not burden them; they see you praying and consider that obligatory upon them. If you must do it, then in the houses."
"4695","From Ath-Thawri, from Ibn Ajlan, from a man called Suhayl, from Al-Hasan ibn Ali, he said: He saw people at the grave and forbade them, and said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not take my house as a festival, and do not take your houses as graves, and send blessings upon me wherever you are, for your blessings reach me."
"4696","From Ath-Thawri, from Nusayr ibn Dhu'luq, he said: I never saw Ar-Rabi' ibn Khuthaym praying voluntarily in the mosque of the quarter at all."
"4697","From Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash, from Ata ibn as-Sa'ib, he said: I saw the best of Ali's companions: Zadham, Maysarah, and Abu al-Bakhtari, preferring the mosque in the month of Ramadan over their families, meaning they stood with the people."
"4698","Ma'mar informed us, from Abu Ghalib, he said: I asked Abu Umamah about the supererogatory prayer, and he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had a supererogatory prayer, and for you it is a merit."
"4699","From Ma'mar, from one who heard Al-Hasan saying: A man said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): O Messenger of Allah, which of the believers is best in faith? He said: The best of them in character. He said: Then which faith is best? He said: Patience and generosity. He said: Then which emigration is best? He said: Whoever abandons what Allah has forbidden him. He said: Then which jihad is best? He said: Whose horse is hamstrung and whose blood is shed. He said: Then which charity is best? He said: The effort of one with little. He said: Then which prayer is best? He said: Lengthy standing. Ma'mar mentioned it from Amr."
"4700","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Ubayd ibn Umayr narrating, he said: It was said: Which jihad is best? He said: Whose horse is hamstrung and whose blood is shed. It was said: Then which prayers are best? He said: Lengthy standing. It was said: Then which charity is best? He said: The effort of one with little. It was said: Then which emigration is best? He said: Whoever abandons what Allah and His Messenger have forbidden him. It was said: Then which of the people is wisest? He said: The one who judges for people as he judges for himself. It was said: Then which of the people is most knowledgeable? He said: The one who gathers the knowledge of people to his own knowledge. He said: I do not know Ubayd except that he raised it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4701","From Ath-Thawri, from Al-A'mash, from Abu Sa'id, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: Which prayer is best? He said: Lengthy standing."
"4702","From Al-Awza'i, from Al-Walid ibn Hisham, from a man, he said: I said to Thawban: Narrate to me a hadith by which Allah may benefit me. I said that to him three times, and he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There is no servant who prostrates to Allah a prostration, except that Allah raises him a degree by it and removes a sin from him by it."
"4703","From Al-Awza'i, from Harun, from Al-Ahnaf ibn Qays, from Abu Dharr, he said: My beloved Abu al-Qasim informed me - then he wept, saying it three times while weeping - then the third time he said: My beloved Abu al-Qasim informed me: There is no servant who prostrates to Allah a prostration, except that Allah raises him a degree by it, removes a sin from him by it, and a good deed is written for him by it."
"4704","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: When Satan sees the son of Adam prostrating, he says: Woe to him; woe to Satan. Allah commanded the son of Adam to prostrate, and he has Paradise, so he obeyed; and He commanded me to prostrate, so I disobeyed, and for me is the Fire."
"4705","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that Abu Hurayrah said: Three things I shall not abandon until I meet Abu al-Qasim: that I sleep every night upon witr, that I fast three days of every month, and the Duha prayer. Ibn Jurayj said: I said to Ata: What if I add to three days? He said: Then it is better."
"4706","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Al-Hasan, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoined upon me three things I shall not leave, neither on journey nor in residence: sleeping upon witr, fasting three days of every month, and the Duha prayer. He said: Then Al-Hasan later erred and put the Friday bath in place of Duha."
"4707","From Isra'il, from Yunus, from Simak ibn Harb, from Abu ar-Rabi', from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoined upon me three things I shall never abandon: that I not sleep except upon witr, the Duha prayer, and fasting three days of every month."
"4708","From Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Mujahid saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray Duha two rak'ahs, and four and six and eight."
"4709","And Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Mu'adhah al-Adawiyyah, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray the Duha prayer four rak'ahs, and add what Allah willed."
"4710","From Ja'far ibn Sulayman, from Aban, from Al-Hasan, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray the Duha prayer. It was said: What is this? He said: A prayer of desire and fear."
"4711","From Ma'mar, from Aban, from Sulayman ibn Qays, from Anbasah ibn Abi Sufyan, from Umm Habibah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays twelve rak'ahs in a day, Allah will build for him a house in Paradise; and whoever builds a mosque, Allah will build for him one more spacious than it."
"4712","From Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Allah said: O son of Adam, are you unable to pray four rak'ahs at the beginning of the day, that I suffice you its end?"
"4713","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed us, from Umm Hani bint Abi Talib, that she entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of the Conquest while he was in a dome of his, and found him having bathed with water that was in a bowl - I indeed see in it traces of dough - and saw him praying Duha."
"4714","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Umm Hani - and he was staying with her - that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was screened, and bathed in the forenoon, then prayed eight rak'ahs; it is not known whether his standing was longer or his bowing or his prostration."
"4715","From Ma'mar, from Az-Zuhri, from Umm Hani, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed eight rak'ahs in Duha, their standing and bowing and prostration being nearly equal."
"4716","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from Al-Muttalib ibn Abdullah ibn Hantab, from Umm Hani, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) alighted on the day of the Conquest at the heights of Makkah, so I came to him. Abu Dharr came to him with a bowl in which was water. She said: I indeed see in it traces of dough. He said: Abu Dharr screened him, so he bathed; then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) screened Abu Dharr, so he bathed; then he prayed eight rak'ahs, and that was in the forenoon."
"4717","From Malik, from Maymun ibn Maysarah, from Abu Murrah, freedman of Aqil, from Umm Hani, he said: I heard her saying: I went to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of the Conquest and found him bathing, with Fatimah his daughter screening him with a garment. I greeted, and that was in the forenoon. He said: Who is this? I said: Umm Hani bint Abi Talib. He said: Welcome to Umm Hani. When he finished his bath, he prayed eight rak'ahs wrapped in a single garment, then departed. I said: O Messenger of Allah, the son of my mother claims he will fight so-and-so son of Umayyah, a man to whom I have given protection. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We have given protection to whom Umm Hani has given protection."
"4718","From Ma'mar, from Az-Zuhri, he said: I asked him about the Duha prayer, and he said: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray at midday - or he said: at midday heat - and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never prayed the Duha prayer at all except on the day of the Conquest of Makkah, and when he returned from a journey."
"4719","From Az-Zuhri, from Abd ar-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned from the expedition of Tabuk to Madinah in the forenoon and prayed two rak'ahs in the mosque. He said: And when he came from a journey he used to do that."
"4720","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me that Abd ar-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik narrated to him, from his father Abdullah ibn Ka'b, and from his uncle Ubaydullah ibn Ka'b, from Ka'b ibn Malik, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not return from a journey except by day in the forenoon; when he returned he began with the mosque and prayed two rak'ahs in it, then sat in it."
"4721","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ja'far ibn Muhammad narrated to me that Ali ibn Abi Talib would be told of this prayer which the people innovated, and he would say: Pray what you are able, for Allah does not punish for prayer."
"4722","From Malik, from Zayd ibn Aslam, that Aishah used to pray Duha eight rak'ahs, and say: Even if my father were raised for me, I would not leave them."
"4723","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, she used to say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not glorify the Duha glorification. He said: And Aishah used to glorify it, and she used to say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to leave an act fearing that people would take it as a practice and it would be made obligatory upon them; and he loved what was light upon people."
"4724","From Ma'mar, from Az-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: Uthman was killed while no one glorified it, and nothing the people innovated is dearer to me than it."
"4725","From Ibn Jurayj or Ma'mar, he said: Ibn Shihab said: Salim ibn Abdullah narrated to me, from Ibn Umar, that he said: Uthman was afflicted while no one glorified it, and it is among the dearest of what people innovated to me. He said: Ibn Jurayj said, and people said: The first to pray it were the desert folk who entered the mosque when they finished their markets."
"4726","From Ma'mar, from Ata al-Khurasani, he said: Ibn Abbas said: Something about the Duha prayer remained in my mind until I recited: We subjected the mountains with him, glorifying in the evening and at sunrise."
"4727","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman al-Ahwal informed me that he heard Ata al-Khurasani saying to Tawus: Ibn Abbas says: The Duha prayer is in the Quran, but none dives for it except a diver. Then he recited: They glorify in the evening and at sunrise. Tawus said: By Allah, Ibn Abbas never prayed it until he died, except when circumambulating the House."
"4728","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman also informed me that he heard Tawus saying: The first to pray it were the Bedouins; when one of them sold merchandise he came to the mosque and magnified and prostrated. Except that Tawus says: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, then the Bedouin prostrates."
"4729","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Ibn Abbas said: The Duha prayer is when the shadows break."
"4730","From Ibn Uyaynah, he said: A shaykh of Bajilah informed me, he said: I heard Ash-Sha'bi saying: Abdullah ibn Mas'ud did not pray Duha, and he prayed between Zuhr and Asr along with a long portion of the night."
"4731","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Mujalid ibn Sa'id, from Ash-Sha'bi, from his uncle Qays ibn Abd, he said: I frequented Abdullah ibn Mas'ud for a year, and I never saw him praying the Duha prayer, nor fasting a day outside Ramadan. He said: While we were with him one night, someone came and it was said to him: This is the messenger of the governor. Abdullah said: Extinguish the lamp. He entered and said to him: The commander says to you: Leave these words which you say. He said: And what are they? He said: These words? He kept repeating them. He said: Your saying: Every innovation is misguidance. He said: I shall never leave them. He said: Then he says to you: Go out. He said: Then I shall go out. He said: So he went out to Madinah. From a man, from Shu'bah, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from his father, from his grandfather, from Abd ar-Rahman ibn Awf, he said: I never saw him pray it."
"4732","From Abd ar-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Salim ibn Abdullah, he said: I said to Abdullah ibn Umar: Why do I not see you praying Duha? He said: I did not see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying it."
"4733","From his father, from Khallad ibn Abd ar-Rahman, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr and Mujahid, they both said: Whoever prays Duha eight rak'ahs is written among the oft-returning; indeed He is Forgiving to the oft-returning."
"4734","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Isma'il, from Ash-Sha'bi, he said: I heard Ibn Umar saying: I have not prayed Duha since I embraced Islam."
"4735","From Ibn at-Taymi, from his father, from Nu'aym ibn Abi Hind, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, that he said concerning a man praying following the imam: If between them is a river or a road or a wall, he should not follow him."
"4736","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Isa, he said: I asked Amir Ash-Sha'bi about a woman praying following the imam with a road between them. He said: That is not for her."
"4737","From Ibn al-Mujalid, from his father, from Ibrahim an-Nakha'i, that he said concerning a man praying following the imam with a wall between them: It is good, so long as there is no road or women between them."
"4738","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Suhayl, from Al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah, that she used to pray following the imam in her house while he was in the mosque."
"4739","From Ibn at-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Mijlaz, he said: A woman may pray following the imam, even if between them is a road or a wall, so long as she hears the takbir; there is no harm."
"4740","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, he said: I came, I and my father once, and found the mosque full, so we prayed following the imam in a house by the mosque with a road between them. From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Hisham ibn Urwah, the like of it, except that he said: We prayed in the house of Humayd ibn Abd ar-Rahman."
"4741","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Suhayl, from Salih ibn Ibrahim, that he saw Anas ibn Malik praying Jumu'ah in the house of Humayd ibn Abd ar-Rahman following Al-Walid ibn Abd al-Malik, with a road between them."
"4742","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Salih, freedman of At-Taw'amah, that he saw Abu Hurayrah praying on the roof of the mosque following the imam while he was beneath him."
"4743","From Ma'mar, from Az-Zuhri, from Abbad ibn Tamim, from his uncle, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out with the people seeking rain, and prayed two rak'ahs with them, reciting aloud in them, and turned his cloak and supplicated and faced the qiblah."
"4744","From Ma'mar and Ath-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from Abbad ibn Tamim, from his uncle, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sought rain, faced the qiblah and turned his cloak."
"4745","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Salih, freedman of At-Taw'amah, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed for rain, and prayed two rak'ahs at the place of prayer."
"4746","From Ma'mar, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Allah laughs at you, distressed, with the nearness of rain to you. A man of Bahilah said: O Messenger of Allah, does our Lord laugh? He said: Yes. He said: By Allah, we shall never lack good from a Lord who laughs."
"4747","From Ath-Thawri, from Ibn Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Kinanah, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: A commander among the commanders sent me to Ibn Abbas to ask him about seeking rain. Ibn Abbas said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out humble, supplicating, abased, and addressed, but did not address like this address of yours; then he supplicated and prayed two rak'ahs as he prays in Eid. Sufyan said: I said: Did he pray before the address or after it? He said: I do not know."
"4748","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Abu al-Huwayrith, from Ibn Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Kinanah, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I think that he used to magnify in Fitr and Adha and seeking rain, seven in the first and five in the last."
"4749","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, he said: Ali used to magnify in Fitr and Adha and seeking rain, seven in the first and five in the other, and pray before the address, and recite aloud. He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Abu Bakr, Umar and Uthman, used to do that."
"4750","From Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The sunnah of seeking rain is like the sunnah of Fitr and Adha in the takbir."
"4751","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he sought rain, turned his right cloak upon his left side and his left upon his right side, then faced the qiblah, then came down and prayed two rak'ahs."
"4752","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Abd ar-Rahman ibn al-Harith narrating: That he attended Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, when he was governor over Madinah, seeking rain upon the pulpit, then he came down and prayed."
"4753","From Ath-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Khatmi, that Ibn az-Zubayr went out seeking rain with the people, and addressed, then prayed without adhan or iqamah. He said: And among the people that day were Al-Bara ibn Azib and Zayd ibn Arqam."
"4754","From Rabah ibn Ubaydullah ibn Umar, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Hunayn informed me, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: He used to recite in the two rak'ahs of seeking rain: By the sun and its brightness, and By the night when it covers."
"4755","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa asked Ata: Is there a prayer in seeking rain? He did not distinguish for him anything from those who passed. Sulayman said: Then he mentioned to us that Umar ibn al-Khattab went out with the people to the place of prayer and supplicated and sought forgiveness, then came down and departed, and did not pray."
"4756","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Mutarrif, from Ash-Sha'bi, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab went out seeking rain with the people, and did not add to seeking forgiveness until he returned. They said: O Commander of the Faithful, we did not see you seek rain. He said: I sought rain with the downpours of heaven by which rain is sought: I said, Seek forgiveness of your Lord, indeed He is Forgiving; He will send the sky upon you in showers, and extend you with wealth and sons; Seek forgiveness of your Lord, then repent to Him; He will send the sky upon you in showers and increase you in strength to your strength."
"4757","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ja'far ibn Burqan, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to Maymun ibn Mihran: I have written to the people of the cities to go out on such a day of such a month to seek rain; and whoever is able to fast and give charity, let him do so. For Allah says: He has succeeded who purifies himself, and remembers the name of his Lord and prays. And say as your two fathers said: Our Lord, we have wronged ourselves, and if You do not forgive us and have mercy upon us, we shall be among the losers. And say as Nuh said: Unless You forgive me and have mercy upon me, I shall be among the losers. And say as Musa said: I have wronged myself, so forgive me; so He forgave him; indeed He is the Forgiving, the Merciful. And say as Yunus, upon him be peace, said: There is no god but You; glory be to You; indeed I have been among the wrongdoers."
"4758","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Husayn ibn Abdullah ibn Dumayrah, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ali, that he said concerning seeking rain: When you go out, praise Allah and extol Him as He deserves, and send blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and seek forgiveness, for seeking rain is seeking forgiveness."
"4759","He said: And Ali said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned his cloak while standing, when he intended to supplicate."
"4760","From Ath-Thawri, from Al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, from Ibn Mas'ud, that a man, while watering his crops, saw a cloud in which was a voice: Give water to the land of so-and-so. He followed the voice until he reached the land that was named, and asked its owner: What is your practice in it? He said: I return a third in it, give a third in charity, and keep a third for my family. From Ath-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, from An-Nakha'i, from Masruq, that Ibn Mas'ud used to send him to his land and command him to do likewise in it."
"4761","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Habib ibn Abi Thabit informed me that it reached him that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, help me against Mudar with famine. A Mudari man came to him and said: O Prophet of Allah, by Allah, no camel of ours moves briskly, nor is a shepherd provisioned for us. He repeated his saying, and he turned away from him. Then he remained as Allah willed. Then he called the Mudari and said: What did you say? He repeated it to him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, I supplicated You and You answered me, and I asked You and You gave me. O Allah, give us a rescuing rain, wholesome, fertile, enveloping, immediate without delay, beneficial without harm. Before evening came, the sky was clothed with clouds and it rained, and no one came from any direction except reporting rain. We said to him: What is yukhtir? He said: It bellows."
"4762","From Ma'mar, from Al-A'mash, that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, Mudar has perished, so seek rain from Allah for them - or he said: supplicate for them. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said at that: O Allah, give us a wholesome, pleasant rain, fertile, enveloping, immediate without delay, beneficial without harm. He said: They remained only a week until the people were revived."
"4763","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Sa'id or another, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, he said: A man stood to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, you supplicated against Mudar with famine, so no camel of theirs grunts for them, nor does a child cry out for them. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood upon the pulpit and said: O Allah, give us a rescuing rain, fertile, blessed, wholesome, beneficial, enveloping, immediate without delay. He said: That day did not pass until they were rained upon - or a week did not pass - until the people abounded in herbage."
"4764","From Ma'mar, from a shaykh of theirs, from Anas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sought rain once, and the people were rained upon three days or as Allah willed, then it did not cease from them. He said: It was said: O Messenger of Allah, the walls have collapsed, the riders are cut off, and we fear drowning. He supplicated and said: O Allah, around us and not upon us. He said: I saw the clouds split from Madinah until it was like a ring from it, while around it was dark; I knew it was rained upon."
"4765","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told from Anas, he said: I attended the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a man came to him and complained to him of drought while he was upon the pulpit, so he sought rain - and he did not mention his speech - and the sky became covered with clouds, and it rained until the next Friday. It was said to him: O Messenger of Allah, we drowned, the cattle perished, and the traveler does not go out. He laughed, then said: O Allah, around us and not upon us. He said: I saw him when the clouds parted from Madinah and dispersed, until I was in a gap from it."
"4766","From Ibn at-Taymi, he said: I heard Yahya ibn Sa'id - I think he mentioned it - from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to seek rain, saying: O Allah, give drink to Your servants and Your beasts, spread Your mercy, and give life to Your dead land."
"4767","He said: And I heard him saying: When the rain was severe, Umar ibn al-Khattab would say: O Allah, keep it from the houses of mud; O Allah, upon the backs of the hills, the bellies of the valleys, and the places where trees grow."
"4768","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Husayn ibn Abdullah, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, that Umar sought rain at the place of prayer and said to Al-Abbas: Stand and seek rain. Al-Abbas stood and said: O Allah, with You are clouds, and with You is water, so spread the clouds, then send down water in them, then send it down upon us, strengthen the root by it, lengthen the crop by it, and flow the udder by it. O Allah, intercede us in ourselves and our families. O Allah, we have interceded to You for those who have no speech: our beasts and our cattle. O Allah, give us a gentle, reaching drink, enveloping, general, life-giving. O Allah, we desire none but You alone, with no partner. O Allah, we complain to You of the hunger of every hungry one, the debt of every debtor, the hunger of every starving one, the nakedness of every naked one, and the fear of every fearful one - in a supplication of his."
"4769","From Ma'mar, from Isma'il Abu al-Miqdam, from Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr, he said: A year of drought struck the people, and a man was in the desert. He went out, prayed two rak'ahs with his companions, and sought rain, then slept. He saw in a dream that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to him and said: Convey greetings to Umar, and inform him that Allah has answered you; and Umar had gone out and sought rain too. And command him to fulfill the covenant and tighten the contract. The man went until he came to Umar and said: Seek permission for the messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Umar heard him and said: Who is this fabricator against the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? The man said: Do not hasten against me, O Commander of the Faithful. He informed him of the news, and Umar wept."
"4770","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Ja'far, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated for a people to be rained upon, but they were not rained upon. He said: I supplicated for you while something was in my soul against you, so you were not rained upon; but now you shall be rained upon. He supplicated for them and they were rained upon."
"4771","From Ma'mar, from a man, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, that Isa son of Maryam went out seeking rain, and went out with the people, then said to them: Whoever among you has committed a sin, let him return. The people kept returning until none remained but a one-eyed man. Isa said to him: Supplicate and I shall say amen. He supplicated, and Isa said amen, and Allah gave them rain."
"4772","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Sulayman ibn Abdullah ibn Uwaymir, from Urwah ibn az-Zubayr, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you sees lightning or rain, let him not point to it, but let him describe it."
"4773","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade pointing to the rain."
"4774","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said one day: Where is Tibnayn? They said: A valley among the valleys of Yemen. He said: This is a cloud commanded to Tibnayn; what does its owner do with it? They said: He divides its fruit into three: a third for him and his family, a third for his charity, and a third he returns into it. He said: All of that is in the path of Allah."
"4775","From Ibn at-Taymi, from Abu Awanah, and from Al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Silence in three places: in Jumu'ah, in seeking rain, and in the two Eids. And Qays ibn ar-Rabi' mentioned it, from Salamah, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, the like of it."
"4776","From Ma'mar, from Az-Zuhri, that Sulayman son of Dawud went out, he and his companions, seeking rain, and saw an ant standing, raising one of its legs, seeking rain. He said to his companions: Return, for you have been given rain; this ant sought rain and was answered."
"4777","From Ma'mar, from Az-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, she said: The sun eclipsed in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and prayed with the people, prolonging the recitation, then bowed prolonging the bowing, then raised his head and prolonged the recitation, shorter than his first recitation, then bowed prolonging the bowing, shorter than his first bowing, then raised his head and prostrated two prostrations. Then he stood and did in the second rak'ah the like of that, then departed and said: The sun and the moon are not eclipsed for the death of anyone nor for his life, but they are two signs among the signs of Allah; when you see that, hasten to prayer. Ma'mar said: And Hisham ibn Urwah informed me, from his father, from Aishah, the like of this, and added, he said: When you see that, give charity and pray."
"4778","From Ath-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Amrah, from Aishah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and prayed with them; he stood and bowed, then raised his head and stood shorter than the first standing, then bowed, then raised his head, then prostrated, then stood and did the like of that in the second, except that its standing and bowing were shorter than the first; in every rak'ah two bowings."
"4779","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Amrah, from Aishah, she said: A Jewish woman came to me and said: May Allah protect you from the punishment of the grave. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, shall we be punished in our graves? He said: The Jews have lied. Then the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rode one day a mount, and the sun eclipsed. She said: I went out with some women, and we were between the stones, when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came from his mount to his place of prayer. He stood a long standing and lengthened, then bowed a long bowing and lengthened his bowing, then raised and stood a long standing, shorter than his first standing, then bowed a long bowing, shorter than his first bowing, then raised, then prostrated a long prostration, then stood a long standing, shorter than his first standing, and did as he did in the first. Then he sat. She said: Then I heard him seeking refuge from the punishment of the grave."
"4780","From Malik, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The sun eclipsed, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with the people with him. He stood a long standing, about Surah al-Baqarah, then bowed a long bowing, then raised and stood a long standing, shorter than the first standing, then bowed a bowing shorter than the first bowing, then prostrated. Then he stood and did in the second rak'ah the like of that, but his standing in it was shorter than the first standing, and his bowing and prostration shorter than what he did in the first rak'ah. Then he departed while the sun had cleared. Then he said: The sun and the moon are two signs among the signs of Allah the Exalted; they are not eclipsed for the death of anyone nor for his life; when you see that, remember Allah. They said: O Messenger of Allah, we saw you take something from this station of yours, then we saw you hold back. He said: I was shown Paradise - or I saw Paradise - and I took a bunch from it; had I taken it, you would have eaten from it as long as the world remained. And I saw the Fire, and I never saw a sight like today at all; I saw most of its people were women. It was said: Why, O Messenger of Allah? He said: For their ingratitude. It was said: Do they disbelieve in Allah? He said: They are ungrateful to the companion and ungrateful for kindness; had you done good to one of them for a lifetime, then she saw something from you, she would say: I never saw any good from you at all."
"4781","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ata saying: I heard Ubayd ibn Umayr saying: One whom I trust informed me - and I thought he meant Aishah - that she said: The sun eclipsed in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He stood with the people a severe standing: he stands with the people then bows, and stands then bows, and stands then bows; he bowed in two rak'ahs, in every rak'ah three bowings: he bows the third then prostrates. He did not depart until the sun cleared, and even men that day were overcome, until water-skins of water were poured upon them from his standing with them. He would say when he bowed: Allah is Greatest, and when he raised: Allah hears him who praises Him. Then he stood and praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: The sun and the moon are not eclipsed for the death of anyone nor for his life, but they are two signs among the signs of Allah by which He frightens you; when they are eclipsed, hasten to the remembrance of Allah until it clears. And it was added upon Ata in this address: But the best may die in the regions of the earth, and the jinn spread that, so there was darkness for that. He said: One other than Ubayd informed me, saying, he said: Paradise and the Fire were shown to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was in his prayer on the day the sun eclipsed, and he stepped back from his place of prayer behind him until the people rode upon one another, saying: O my Lord, and I; O my Lord, and I. Then he returned walking until he returned to his place of prayer. He saw, when the Fire was shown to him, Abu Khuza'ah Amr ibn Luhayy in the Fire dragging his intestines. He said: They claimed he used to rob the pilgrims with a hooked staff of his, saying: O my Lord, I do not steal; only my hooked staff steals. He said: And the owner of the cat: a woman tied it and did not feed it, nor let it go, nor give it drink, so it eats and drinks."
"4782","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Mansur ibn Abd ar-Rahman informed me, from his mother Safiyyah bint Shaybah, from Asma bint Abi Bakr, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out on the day the sun eclipsed, and took a coat of mail and wore it until he was reached with his cloak. He stood with the people a long standing: he stands then bows; had a man come after he bowed, he would not have known that he bowed at all, never imagining that he bowed, from the length of the standing. She said: I began looking at the woman who was older, and at the woman who was sicker than me, standing, and saying: I am more entitled to endure the length of standing than you."
"4783","From Bakkar, from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah, from Ya'la ibn Hakim, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, that Ibn Abbas recited in the first rak'ah in the eclipse: Al-Hamd, and Al-Baqarah; and in the second: Al-Hamd, and Al Imran."
"4784","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah and Asim al-Ahwal, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Ibn Abbas, that he prayed during the earthquake in Basra, prolonging the standing, then bowed, then raised his head and prolonged the standing, then bowed, then bowed, then prostrated, then prayed the second likewise; so his prayer became three bowings and four prostrations. And he said: Such is the prayer of the signs. And Ma'mar said: One of our companions informed me that Ibn Abbas recited in the first rak'ah Al-Baqarah, and in the last Al Imran."
"4785","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: Hudhayfah prayed in Al-Mada'in with his companions like the prayer of Ibn Abbas concerning the signs."
"4786","From al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha' or 'Asim al-Ahwal, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Ibn 'Abbas, that he prayed during the earthquake in Basra, and they agreed that he performed six bowings in two rak'as, three in each rak'a, but they differed: 'Asim said, ""He recited between every two bowings,"" and Khalid said, ""He recited in the first of each rak'a thereof."" Then afterwards it came back from al-Thawri, who said: Hisham informed me, from a man, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Ibn 'Abbas, that when he prayed with them, he said, ""Such is the prayer of the signs."""
"4787","From Bakkar, from 'Abd al-Karim Abu Umayya, from Ya'la ibn Hakim, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, that he recited in the first rak'a Surah al-Baqarah, and in the last one Al 'Imran. And Ma'mar mentioned it from Ibn 'Abbas."
"4788","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Sulayman al-Ahwal informed me that Tawus informed him that Ibn 'Abbas, when the sun eclipsed, prayed on the back of the terrace of Zamzam two rak'as, in each rak'a four bowings."
"4789","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, that he prayed for the eclipse of the sun: he recited, then bowed four bowings in each prostration-cycle, except that when he raised his head from bowing, he recited, then prostrated, then stood and did in the second the same as he did in the first."
"4790","From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from al-Hakam, from Hanash, from 'Ali, that he led the people in the mosque for the eclipse of the sun. He said: He recited aloud, stood and recited, then bowed, then stood and supplicated, then bowed four bowings in one prostration-cycle, supplicating in them after bowing, then did the same in the second. Sufyan said: I heard them estimating 'Ali's standing in recitation as the length of al-Rum, or Ya Sin, or al-'Ankabut."
"4791","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, who said: The prayer for the eclipse of the sun and the moon is two rak'as, similar to our prayer."
"4792","From al-Thawri, from 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, from his father, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with them on the day the sun eclipsed, the day his son Ibrahim died. He stood with the people, and it was said: he will not bow, but he bowed; it was said: he will not rise, but he rose; it was said: he will not prostrate, but he prostrated; it was said: he will not rise, but he sat; it was said: he will not prostrate, but he prostrated; it was said: he will not rise. Then he stood for the second and did the same, and the sun cleared."
"4793","From Isma'il ibn 'Abdullah, who said: Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'ida narrated to me, from al-Sha'bi, who said: While al-Mughira ibn Shu'ba was over Kufa, the sun eclipsed, so he stood and prayed with the people. I was where I could not hear, so I estimated it as the length of a surah of the two-hundreds, then he bowed, then rose, recited, then bowed, then the sun cleared, so he bowed and prostrated, then stood for the second, recited a light recitation, then bowed and prostrated."
"4794","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: A man said to 'Ata': What do you think, if the moon eclipses, do I pray as the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed when the sun eclipsed? He said: Yes, except that it is not a congregational prayer."
"4795","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: 'Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from 'Ikrima, the freedman of Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The moon eclipsed in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they said: The moon has been bewitched. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The Hour has drawn near, and the moon has split,"" up to ""continuing."""
"4796","From Ma'mar, who said: I asked al-Zuhri about a sign occurring after 'Asr. He said: Supplication, and there is no prayer in it after 'Asr. I said: From whom do you narrate? He said: That is what they used to do."
"4797","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, who said: The sun and the moon do not eclipse for anyone's death or life, but when our Lord, blessed and exalted is He, manifests Himself to one of His creation, it humbles itself before Him."
"4798","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), whenever he bowed a bowing and raised his head, sent a man to look whether it had cleared."
"4799","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who used to say: From where did the people take the qunut? And he expressed amazement, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only performed qunut for some days, then left that."
"4800","From 'Abdullah ibn Muharrar, from al-Zuhri, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, as did Abu Bakr and 'Umar, while they did not perform qunut."
"4801","From Ma'mar, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from 'Alqama and al-Aswad, that they both said: 'Umar led us in prayer for a time without performing qunut."
"4802","From al-Thawri, from Mansur and al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid and 'Amr ibn Maymun al-Awdi, who said: We prayed Fajr behind 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he did not perform qunut."
"4803","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Alqama ibn Qays, that Ibn Mas'ud did not perform qunut in the Fajr prayer."
"4804","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', that Ibn 'Umar did not perform qunut in the morning prayer, nor in witr either."
"4805","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn 'Uthman al-Taymi, who said: I heard 'Amr ibn Maymun say: I prayed Fajr behind 'Umar, and he did not perform qunut in it."
"4806","From Malik, from Nafi', that Ibn 'Umar did not perform qunut in Fajr."
"4807","From Hushaym, from a man he named - I think he said Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman - that Ibn 'Abbas prayed the morning prayer and did not perform qunut."
"4808","Ibn al-Mujalid said, from his father, from Ibrahim, from 'Alqama and al-Aswad, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not perform qunut in any of the prayers except when he was at war, for he would perform qunut in all the prayers; and neither Abu Bakr, nor 'Umar, nor 'Uthman performed qunut until they died; even 'Ali did not perform qunut until he fought the people of Syria, when he would perform qunut in all the prayers. And Mu'awiya would also perform qunut, each one of them supplicating against the other."
"4809","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, and al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Abu al-Sha'tha', who said: I asked Ibn 'Umar about qunut in Fajr, and he said: I did not know that anyone does it."
"4810","From Ibn 'Uyayna, from Ibn Abi Najih, who said: I asked Salim ibn 'Abdullah: Did 'Umar ibn al-Khattab perform qunut in the morning prayer? He said: No; it is only something people introduced afterwards."
"4811","From Ibn 'Uyayna, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Muslim ibn Subayh, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, who said: 'Umar did not perform qunut in the morning prayer."
"4812","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: One who heard Ibn 'Abbas and Muhammad ibn 'Ali at al-Khayf informed me, both saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would perform qunut with these words in the morning prayer and in the witr at night: O Allah, guide me among those You have guided, grant me well-being among those You have granted well-being, befriend me among those You have befriended, bless for me what You have given, and shield me from the evil of what You have decreed. Indeed You decree and none decrees upon You, and indeed whoever You befriend is not humiliated. Blessed are You, our Lord, and exalted."
"4813","From al-Thawri, from Nusayr ibn Dhu'luq, who said: I prayed behind Rabi' ibn Khuthaym, and he performed qunut before bowing."
"4814","From al-Thawri, from Makhariq, from Tariq ibn Shihab, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab prayed the morning prayer, and when he finished the recitation he performed qunut, then said takbir when bowing."
"4815","From al-Thawri, from 'Abd al-A'la, from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, that 'Ali said takbir when he performed qunut in Fajr, then said takbir when bowing."
"4816","From al-Thawri, from Mutarrif ibn Tarif, from Abu al-Jahm, from al-Bara' ibn 'Azib, that he performed qunut in Fajr, saying takbir when he finished the recitation, then saying takbir when he finished the qunut."
"4817","From Abu Ja'far, from Qatada, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed qunut in the Fajr prayer, as did Abu Bakr and 'Umar, after bowing; and when it was 'Uthman's time, he performed qunut before bowing so that the people could catch the rak'a."
"4818","From Abu Ja'far, from 'Asim, from Anas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed qunut in the morning after bowing, supplicating against tribes of the Arabs; and his qunut before that and after it was before bowing."
"4819","From Abu Ja'far, from al-Rabi' ibn Anas, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) continued to perform qunut in Fajr until he departed this world."
"4820","From 'Uthman ibn Matar, who said: Hanzala narrated to me that he heard Anas say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed qunut in Fajr after bowing."
"4821","From Abu Ja'far, from Humayd, from Anas, who said: I said to him: How did you perform qunut? He said: All of that was before bowing and after it."
"4822","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Alqama and al-Aswad, that Ibn Mas'ud did not perform qunut in the morning prayer."
"4823","From Ma'mar, from 'Ali ibn Zayd ibn Jud'an, from Abu Rafi', who said: I prayed the morning prayer behind 'Umar ibn al-Khattab and he performed qunut after bowing. He said: I heard him say: O Allah, we seek Your help, seek Your forgiveness, praise You and do not disbelieve in You; we believe in You, and we renounce and abandon whoever disobeys You. O Allah, You alone we worship, to You we pray and prostrate, to You we hasten and hurry; we hope for Your mercy and fear Your punishment; indeed Your punishment overtakes the disbelievers. O Allah, punish the disbelievers, cast terror into their hearts, divide their unity, and send down upon them Your wrath and punishment. O Allah, punish the disbelievers among the People of the Book who bar from Your path, belie Your messengers, and fight Your allies. O Allah, forgive the believing men and women, the Muslim men and women, set right their relations, join their hearts, place faith and wisdom in their hearts, keep them firm upon the creed of Your Prophet, inspire them to fulfill the covenant You made with them, grant them victory over Your enemy and theirs, O God of truth, and make us among them. 'Abd al-Razzaq said: If I were an imam I would say these words, then I would say: O Allah, guide us among those You have guided."
"4824","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: 'Ata' informed me that he heard 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr transmitting from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab regarding the qunut: that he used to say: O Allah, forgive the believing men and women, the Muslim men and women, join their hearts, set right their relations, grant them victory over Your enemy and theirs. O Allah, curse the disbelievers among the People of the Book who belie Your messengers and fight Your allies. O Allah, divide their unity, shake their feet, and send upon them Your might which You do not turn away from the criminal people. In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate: O Allah, we seek Your help, seek Your forgiveness, praise You and do not disbelieve in You, and we renounce and abandon whoever disobeys You. In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate: O Allah, You alone we worship, to You we pray and prostrate, to You we hasten and hurry; we hope for Your mercy and fear Your punishment; indeed Your punishment overtakes the disbelievers. He said: And I heard 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr say: The qunut is before the last rak'a of the morning prayer. And it was mentioned that it reached him that they are two surahs of the Qur'an in the codex of Ibn Mas'ud, and that he would perform witr with them every night. And it was mentioned that he would recite the qunut aloud in the morning. I said: Yet you dislike seeking forgiveness in the obligatory prayer, while here 'Umar sought forgiveness. He said: He had finished; it is in the supplication at its end."
"4825","From al-Thawri, from Ja'far ibn Burqan, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, that he used to say: O Allah, we seek Your help, seek Your forgiveness, praise You and do not disbelieve in You; we renounce and abandon whoever disobeys You. O Allah, You alone we worship, to You we pray and prostrate, to You we hasten and hurry; we fear Your punishment and hope for Your mercy; indeed Your punishment overtakes the disbelievers."
"4826","From Mubarak, from 'Asim ibn Sulayman, from Abu 'Uthman al-Nahdi, that 'Umar would perform qunut in the morning prayer to the length of a hundred verses of the Qur'an."
"4827","From a man, from Shu'ba, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn 'Abbas, that 'Umar would perform qunut in Fajr with two surahs."
"4828","From Ja'far, from 'Awf, who said: Abu Raja' al-'Utaridi narrated to me, saying: Ibn 'Abbas led us in the morning prayer during his governorship over Basra, and he performed qunut before bowing."
"4829","From Ja'far, from 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, from 'Abdullah ibn Habib, that 'Ali would perform qunut in the morning prayer before bowing, and in witr before bowing. He said: And 'Awf informed me that 'Ali would perform qunut before bowing."
"4830","Al-Thawri informed us, from 'Amr ibn Murra, who said: I heard 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla narrate from al-Bara' ibn 'Azib that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would perform qunut in Fajr and Maghrib."
"4831","From Yahya, from al-Thawri, from Salama ibn Kuhayl, from 'Abdullah ibn Ma'qil, that 'Ali performed qunut in Maghrib, supplicating against some people and their partisans, and he performed qunut before bowing."
"4832","From Ibn 'Uyayna, from 'Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq, who said: I saw al-Hasan meet Abu Rafi' the goldsmith and say: I am between the two views. Al-Hasan said: The qunut is before bowing. Abu Rafi' said: No, after bowing; we did it with 'Umar. Al-Hasan said: For how long? He said: Two months. Abu Rafi' said: Rather two years. He said: And 'Abd al-Karim pointed with his finger, meaning in the morning prayer."
"4833","From al-Hasan ibn 'Umara, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad al-Kahili, that 'Ali would perform qunut with these two surahs in Fajr, except that he would put the last one first, saying: O Allah, You alone we worship, to You we pray and prostrate, to You we hasten and hurry; we hope for Your mercy and fear Your punishment; indeed Your punishment overtakes the disbelievers. O Allah, we seek Your help, seek Your guidance, praise You with all good, thank You and do not disbelieve in You; we believe in You, and we renounce and abandon whoever disobeys You."
"4834","Al-Hakam said: And Tawus informed me that he heard Ibn 'Abbas say: 'Umar performed qunut before bowing with these two surahs, except that he put first what 'Ali put last, and put last what 'Ali put first, and the wording is the same."
"4835","From Ibn 'Uyayna, from al-Mukhariq, who said: I heard Tariq ibn Shihab say: 'Umar performed qunut. He said: Our companions informed me from al-Mukhariq, from Tariq, that he said takbir when he performed qunut, saying it when he finished the recitation, then said takbir when he went down."
"4836","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Sa'id, from Qatada, from Abu Rafi', both saying: We prayed Fajr behind 'Umar and he performed qunut after bowing. One of them said: He raised his hands; the other said: He did not raise his hands."
"4837","From 'Umar ibn Rashid, or another, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, that he would perform qunut in the last rak'a of the Zuhr prayer, the last 'Isha' prayer, and the morning prayer, after saying: Allah hears whoever praises Him; supplicating for the believers and cursing the disbelievers. And he mentions that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do it."
"4838","From Ma'mar, from 'Amr, from al-Hasan, saying: The qunut in witr and the morning is: O Allah, we seek Your help, seek Your forgiveness, praise You with good, do not disbelieve in You, believe in You, and renounce and abandon whoever disobeys You. O Allah, You alone we worship, to You we pray and prostrate, to You we hasten and hurry; we hope for Your mercy and fear Your severe punishment; indeed Your severe punishment overtakes the disbelievers. O Allah, punish the disbelievers and the polytheists, cast terror into their hearts, divide their unity, send down upon them Your wrath and punishment. O Allah, punish the disbelievers among the People of the Book who bar from Your path and belie Your messengers. O Allah, forgive the believing men and women, the Muslim men and women. O Allah, set right their relations, join their hearts, place faith and wisdom in their hearts, inspire them to thank the favor You bestowed upon them, to fulfill the covenant You made with them, grant them victory over Your enemy and theirs, O God of truth, and make us among them. He used to say this, then fall prostrate, and he would not add to this anything of blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And some of those who asked him would say: O Abu Sa'id, does one add to this anything of blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), supplication, glorification, and takbir? He would say: I do not forbid you, but I heard the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) add nothing to this, and he would become angry when they wanted him to add."
"4839","From Ibn Jurayj, and Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: Qunut is but obedience to Allah. And he would perform qunut with four verses from the beginning of al-Baqarah, then: Indeed in the creation of the heavens and the earth - this verse - and: Allah, there is no god but He, the Living, the Sustainer, and this verse: To Him belongs what is in the heavens and what is on earth, until he sealed al-Baqarah; then: Say, He is Allah, the One; then: Say, I seek refuge with the Lord of the daybreak; then: Say, I seek refuge with the Lord of mankind. Then he would say: O Allah, You alone we worship, to You we pray and prostrate, to You we hasten and hurry; we fear Your punishment and hope for Your mercy; indeed Your punishment overtakes the disbelievers. O Allah, we seek Your help, seek Your forgiveness, praise You and do not disbelieve in You; we believe in You, and we renounce and abandon whoever disbelieves in You. And they mentioned that they are two surahs of al-Baqarah, and that their place is after: Say, He is Allah, the One. Ibn Jurayj said in his hadith from Ibn Tawus: My father used to say them in the morning prayer, and he would not recite them aloud; and he would say them in Zuhr, 'Asr, and the last 'Isha', saying in the last two rak'as of Zuhr, 'Asr, and 'Isha' - saying in the first of the last two of Zuhr what is in al-Baqarah, and in the last of the last two of Zuhr what is besides that, and likewise in 'Asr and the last 'Isha'. And he would perform witr, and he would place the recitation in the witr."
"4840","From al-Hasan ibn 'Umara, who said: Burayd ibn Abi Maryam informed me, from Abu al-Hawra', who said: I said to al-Hasan ibn 'Ali: What were you like on the day the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, and what do you remember from him? He said: I remember that a man came to him one day and asked him about something, and he said: Leave what puts doubt in you for what does not put doubt in you, for evil puts doubt in you, while good is tranquility."
